const QUESTION_BANK = [

  // ---------- Momentum and Impulse ----------
  { id: 'mom-01', topic: 'momentum', level: 1, q: 'A hornbill and a supply cart have equal momentum but travel in opposite directions along a vine bridge. What must a complete comparison include?', answers: ['Both magnitude and direction, because momentum is the vector product mv.', 'Only their speeds, because equal momentum makes direction irrelevant.', 'Only the forces driving them, because momentum is force multiplied by time.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'mom-02', topic: 'momentum', level: 1, q: 'A vine delivers a brief force that brings a swinging explorer to rest. Which interpretation links the interaction to the explorer’s motion?', answers: ['The vine’s impulse equals the explorer’s change in momentum.', 'The vine supplies the momentum the explorer possessed before stopping.', 'The stopping force alone is the impulse, regardless of how long it acts.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'mom-03', topic: 'momentum', level: 1, q: 'Jamnani records the force on a raft during a collision as a force–time graph. Which feature determines the raft’s momentum change?', answers: ['The area under the graph, because it is the impulse Δp.', 'The maximum height alone, because peak force equals impulse.', 'The final gradient, because acceleration is the area under a force–time graph.'], correct: 0 },
  {"id":"mom-04","topic":"momentum","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-momentum","q":"An object arrives at the same velocity and stops on either moss or stone. Why can a longer stopping time reduce its average contact force?","answers":["A longer stopping time lowers average force for the same momentum change.","A longer stopping time reduces the impulse for the same momentum change.","A longer stopping time reduces initial momentum before contact begins."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The moss extends the collision time, so the same momentum change occurs with a smaller average force.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"mom-05","topic":"momentum","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-momentum","q":"A ball rebounds normally from a wall at unchanged speed. Why is its momentum change nonzero?","answers":["Reversal makes the vector change larger than the difference in speeds suggests.","Reversal leaves momentum unchanged because the speed magnitude is unchanged.","Reversal makes final momentum zero because the two velocity directions cancel."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The initial and final momenta point in opposite directions, so the change is the sum of their magnitudes.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"mom-06","topic":"momentum","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-momentum","q":"Why can internal forces never change a system’s total momentum?","answers":["Internal force pairs cancel when momentum changes of all parts are added.","Internal forces cannot accelerate individual parts of a fixed-mass system.","Internal forces cancel only when interacting objects have identical masses."],"correct":0,"explanation":"They act in equal and opposite Newton’s-third-law pairs, which cancel for the system as a whole.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  { id: 'mom-07', topic: 'momentum', level: 2, q: 'Newton’s second law in momentum form says F_net = Δp/Δt. What follows when the net force is zero?', answers: ['Momentum stays constant — the object may still be moving, but its momentum cannot change.', 'The object must be stationary, since zero force means no motion.', 'Momentum steadily decreases to zero through friction.'], correct: 0 },
  {"id":"mom-08","topic":"momentum","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-momentum","q":"A flexible cage stops a passenger over a longer time than a rigid cage, with the same initial and final velocities. Explain the effect on average force.","answers":["Deformation lengthens stopping time, reducing force for the same impulse.","Deformation reduces the required impulse even with unchanged end velocities.","Deformation makes the stopping force constant regardless of collision speed."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The cage increases the stopping time, so the same momentum change requires a smaller average force.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"mom-09","topic":"momentum","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-momentum","q":"A firework shell explodes mid-flight. Discuss what happens to the centre of mass (ignore air resistance).","answers":["It retains its projectile trajectory because the explosion adds no external impulse.","It follows the largest fragment because that fragment carries most of the mass.","It loses horizontal velocity because the fragments spread in different directions."],"correct":0,"explanation":"It continues on the original projectile path — the explosion forces are internal, so only gravity moves the centre of mass.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"mom-10","topic":"momentum","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-momentum","q":"In isolated trials, two objects either rebound elastically or stick together. Compare conservation of momentum and kinetic energy.","answers":["Both conserve momentum; only the elastic trial also conserves kinetic energy.","Both conserve kinetic energy; only the elastic trial also conserves momentum.","Both conserve momentum and kinetic energy because neither has external impulse."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Total momentum is conserved in both isolated trials; kinetic energy is conserved only in the elastic rebound, while locking transfers some to other forms.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"mom-11","topic":"momentum","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-momentum","q":"Two skaters initially at rest push apart on frictionless horizontal ice. What happens to their combined centre of mass?","answers":["The centre of mass stays at rest because total horizontal momentum stays zero.","The centre of mass moves toward the lighter skater because that skater is faster.","The centre of mass moves toward the heavier skater because that skater pushes harder."],"correct":0,"explanation":"It stays where it is: the skaters gain equal and opposite momenta, so the total momentum — and the centre-of-mass motion — remains zero.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"mom-12","topic":"momentum","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-momentum","q":"Two objects collide obliquely on a frictionless horizontal surface. Why must total momentum be conserved separately in both horizontal coordinate directions?","answers":["Each component is conserved because net external impulse vanishes in each direction.","Only momentum magnitude is conserved; its direction can change during the collision.","Only the direction of initial motion conserves momentum; transverse momentum need not."],"correct":0,"explanation":"With no net external force there is nothing that can change the total momentum — internal pairs cancel.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},

  // ---------- Energy and Work ----------
  { id: 'ene-01', topic: 'energy', level: 1, q: 'A porter carries a supply crate horizontally at constant height through the canopy. Why does the upward support force do no work on the crate?', answers: ['The force is perpendicular to the horizontal displacement, so Fd cos 90° = 0.', 'The support force is smaller than the crate’s weight, so it cannot transfer energy.', 'The crate moves too slowly for the support force to perform work.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ene-02', topic: 'energy', level: 1, q: 'A falling jungle fruit loses gravitational potential energy and warms the ground when it lands. Which account is consistent with energy conservation?', answers: ['Energy is transferred among gravitational, kinetic, thermal and sound forms; it is not created or destroyed.', 'Kinetic energy is created during the fall and then disappears into the ground.', 'Friction destroys mechanical energy, so total energy decreases after impact.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ene-03', topic: 'energy', level: 1, q: 'For a frictionless vine swing, which energies must be tracked to test whether its mechanical energy remains constant?', answers: ['Its kinetic and gravitational potential energies.', 'Its kinetic and thermal energies only.', 'Every energy form in the jungle, including chemical and sound energy.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ene-04', topic: 'energy', level: 2, q: 'Explain why doubling an object’s speed quadruples its kinetic energy.', answers: ['Kinetic energy depends on v², so twice the speed gives four times the energy.', 'Doubling the speed doubles the mass effectively involved.', 'Speed enters the energy formula twice — once for distance, once for time.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ene-05', topic: 'energy', level: 2, q: 'Why does the work formula include cos θ?', answers: ['Only the component of force along the displacement transfers energy; the perpendicular component does no work.', 'The angle corrects the force for the object’s weight.', 'cos θ converts force into power along the slope.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ene-06', topic: 'energy', level: 2, q: 'A fruit falls from a branch, ignoring drag. Discuss its energy through the fall.', answers: ['Gravitational potential energy converts to kinetic energy, and the total mechanical energy stays constant.', 'Kinetic energy is created new at each moment, increasing the total.', 'Potential energy is destroyed on release and replaced by downward force.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ene-07', topic: 'energy', level: 2, q: 'Why are energy methods preferred when forces vary or height changes?', answers: ['Energy conservation links the start and end states directly, without needing the force at every point along the way.', 'Because energy is conserved, no calculation is needed at all.', 'Forces do not act on objects that change height.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ene-08', topic: 'energy', level: 2, q: 'An explorer holds a heavy relic motionless above a pressure plate. Explain the mechanical work done by the explorer’s upward force on the relic.', answers: ['It is zero because the relic has no displacement, despite the large force and the explorer’s biological energy use.', 'It is large because work increases whenever a force is maintained for a long time.', 'It equals the relic’s weight multiplied by the time it is held.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ene-09', topic: 'energy', level: 3, q: 'A spring is stretched twice as far as before. Justify what happens to the stored elastic energy.', answers: ['It quadruples — elastic energy depends on the square of the extension.', 'It doubles, because energy scales with extension.', 'It stays the same, since the spring constant has not changed.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ene-10', topic: 'energy', level: 3, q: 'A ball bounces on concrete but never returns to its drop height. Explain why.', answers: ['At each bounce some kinetic energy transfers to thermal and sound energy, so less remains as potential energy.', 'Gravity weakens each time the ball rises, capping its height.', 'The ball stores a fixed energy tax in the concrete on every impact.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ene-11', topic: 'energy', level: 3, q: 'A slider descends a frictionless slide. Compare its energies at top and bottom.', answers: ['The potential energy lost (mgΔh) equals the kinetic energy gained — mechanical energy is conserved.', 'Kinetic energy at the bottom exceeds the potential energy lost, since speed adds extra.', 'Potential energy at the top exceeds kinetic energy at the bottom, because gravity resists the descent.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ene-12', topic: 'energy', level: 3, q: 'Why is gravitational potential energy written as a change, mgΔh, rather than an absolute amount?', answers: ['Only differences between heights have physical meaning — the zero level is an arbitrary choice of reference.', 'Because g changes continuously with height in every problem.', 'Because mass varies slightly with position above the ground.'], correct: 0 },

  // ---------- Centre of Mass ----------
  { id: 'com-01', topic: 'com', level: 1, q: 'A strangely shaped idol tumbles through the air. Why is marking its centre of mass useful when analysing the overall motion?', answers: ['Its total mass can be treated as concentrated there for translational motion, even while the idol rotates.', 'It identifies the idol’s densest piece, which must follow a straight path.', 'It gives the geometric centre, which is fixed at the same point for every object.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'com-02', topic: 'com', level: 1, q: 'A uniform stone ring hangs around a temple opening. Where must its centre of mass lie, and what does this reveal?', answers: ['At the centre of the hole, showing that a centre of mass need not lie within the object’s material.', 'Within the stone band, because centre of mass must coincide with matter.', 'Nowhere, because hollow objects cannot have a defined centre of mass.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'com-03', topic: 'com', level: 1, q: 'A thrown jungle staff both travels across a clearing and spins. How should these motions be separated for analysis?', answers: ['As translation of its centre of mass plus rotation about the centre of mass.', 'As translation only, because rotation does not affect the motion of its parts.', 'As rotation only, because every point on the staff traces a circle.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'com-04', topic: 'com', level: 1, q: 'A leaning idol begins to topple when its support can no longer provide a balancing torque. Through which point should its weight be modelled as acting?', answers: ['Through its centre of mass.', 'Through its lowest point, because gravity always acts nearest the ground.', 'Through the edge of its base that remains in contact.'], correct: 0 },
  {"id":"com-05","topic":"com","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-com","q":"Why is the centre of mass a mass-weighted average position?","answers":["Each coordinate contributes in proportion to the mass located at that position.","Each coordinate contributes equally because a centre is a geometric midpoint.","Each coordinate contributes in proportion to speed rather than the mass there."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Heavier pieces pull the average toward their own side — each position counts in proportion to its mass.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  { id: 'com-06', topic: 'com', level: 2, q: 'If the net external force on a system is zero, the centre of mass…', answers: ['remains at rest or moves with constant velocity.', 'must remain at rest — motion is impossible without force.', 'accelerates uniformly in the direction of the largest internal force.'], correct: 0 },
  {"id":"com-07","topic":"com","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-com","q":"For a fixed-mass system, why can internal forces not change its centre-of-mass velocity?","answers":["They can rearrange the parts but cannot accelerate the system centre of mass.","They can accelerate the centre of mass only if different parts have unequal masses.","They can move individual parts only if the centre of mass accelerates with them."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Internal force pairs cancel in the total force sum. They can rearrange the system, but only net external force accelerates its centre of mass; an already moving centre need not stop.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"com-08","topic":"com","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-com","q":"Monkeys scramble in different directions across a drifting raft. Use F_net,external = M_total a_COM to explain which motion can change the raft–monkey system’s centre-of-mass velocity.","answers":["Only external resultant force changes the velocity of the combined centre of mass.","The strongest internal push sets the acceleration of the combined centre of mass.","All parts must share one velocity because the system has one centre-of-mass velocity."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Only a net external force can accelerate the centre of mass; the monkeys’ internal forces can rearrange the system but cannot change its total momentum.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"com-09","topic":"com","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-com","q":"An airborne object breaks into unequal fragments. With negligible air resistance, which construction still gives the centre-of-mass position at each instant?","answers":["Mass-weighted fragment coordinates still locate the unfragmented projectile trajectory.","The geometric midpoint of the fragments always locates the original projectile trajectory.","The fastest fragment locates the centre of mass because it travels farthest after breakup."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Use the mass-weighted mean of fragment positions, not an unweighted midpoint. Internal breakup forces leave the combined centre responding to gravity along the original projectile trajectory.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"com-10","topic":"com","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-com","q":"A rocket expels gas in deep space with negligible external force. Describe the centre of mass of the complete rocket-plus-expelled-gas system.","answers":["Rocket and expelled gas exchange momentum while their combined centre moves uniformly.","Rocket and expelled gas both gain forward momentum from the released chemical energy.","The rocket alone conserves momentum because expelled gas is no longer part of the rocket."],"correct":0,"explanation":"It does not accelerate — the expulsion is an internal force pair, so the system’s centre of mass keeps its original motion.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"com-11","topic":"com","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-com","q":"For a fixed-mass colliding system with negligible external impulse, why is v_COM = total momentum / total mass useful?","answers":["Negligible external impulse keeps total momentum and centre-of-mass velocity fixed.","Negligible external impulse keeps each object's momentum and velocity individually fixed.","Negligible external impulse keeps total kinetic energy and relative velocities fixed."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Momentum conservation keeps v_COM constant through the collision, so it tracks the system’s unchanging bulk motion.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"com-12","topic":"com","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-com","q":"Two masses sit on a light rod, one heavy and one light. Discuss where the centre of mass lies.","answers":["It lies toward the heavier mass because equal opposing moments need unequal distances.","It lies halfway between the masses because the connecting rod has negligible mass.","It lies toward the lighter mass because that mass needs a shorter balancing lever arm."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The mass-weighted mean lies between the two masses. Taking moments about that point gives m_heavy d_heavy = m_light d_light, so the heavier mass has the shorter distance.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},

  // ---------- Relative Velocity ----------
  { id: 'rel-01', topic: 'relative', level: 1, q: 'A canoeist describes a crocodile’s motion differently from an observer on the riverbank. What quantity accounts for the canoeist’s observation?', answers: ['The crocodile’s velocity measured relative to the canoeist’s frame.', 'The positive difference between their speeds, with direction discarded.', 'The riverbank velocity, which is the only physically valid motion.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rel-02', topic: 'relative', level: 1, q: 'A raft A and canoe B move along the same river. Which expression gives the canoe’s velocity as measured by an observer on the raft?', answers: ['v_BA = v_B − v_A.', 'v_BA = v_A − v_B.', 'v_BA = v_A + v_B for every choice of direction.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rel-03', topic: 'relative', level: 1, q: 'A scout on canoe A measures canoe B, while a scout on B measures A. How must their velocity measurements be related?', answers: ['They have equal magnitudes and opposite directions: v_BA = −v_AB.', 'They are identical vectors because both scouts observe the same two canoes.', 'Their magnitudes need not match because each scout occupies a different frame.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rel-04', topic: 'relative', level: 1, q: 'Jamnani reports that a parrot is stationary while an explorer sees it moving backward. What must differ between their descriptions?', answers: ['Their frames of reference—the viewpoints relative to which position and velocity are measured.', 'The force on the parrot, because a frame of reference is a physical interaction.', 'The parrot’s true absolute velocity, which only one observer can measure.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rel-05', topic: 'relative', level: 2, q: 'Two cars travel the same way at similar speeds. Explain what each driver sees.', answers: ['The other car drifts slowly, because their relative velocity is the small difference of nearly equal velocities.', 'The other car appears stationary, because matching direction cancels all motion.', 'The other car approaches rapidly, since their speeds add.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rel-06', topic: 'relative', level: 2, q: 'Why does rain appear to slant toward you when you run through it?', answers: ['In your frame, your own backward velocity is added to the drops, so they meet you at an angle.', 'The raindrops speed up horizontally to match your motion.', 'Running changes the direction gravity acts on the drops.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rel-07', topic: 'relative', level: 2, q: 'Two porters walk directly toward each other. Explain why their closing speed is the sum of their speeds.', answers: ['Their velocities have opposite signs, so subtracting them in v_BA leaves a large magnitude.', 'Speeds always add whenever two people are visible to each other.', 'The ground frame cancels one of the velocities, doubling the other.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rel-08', topic: 'relative', level: 2, q: 'A passenger sits still on a smooth train. Discuss whether they are moving.', answers: ['They are at rest in the train’s frame but moving relative to the ground — motion depends on the frame chosen.', 'They are absolutely at rest, because they feel no motion.', 'They are absolutely moving, because the train is absolutely moving.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rel-09', topic: 'relative', level: 3, q: 'A crocodile drifts at 2 m/s while a canoe paddles at 7 m/s in the same direction. Describe the crocodile’s motion as seen from the canoe.', answers: ['It moves backward at 5 m/s — the slower object falls behind in the faster object’s frame.', 'It moves ahead at 9 m/s, because the two speeds add.', 'It appears motionless, since both move the same way.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rel-10', topic: 'relative', level: 3, q: 'Why does the order of subscripts matter in v_BA = v_B − v_A?', answers: ['Reversing the order reverses the subtraction, flipping the sign of the result.', 'The order is pure convention and changes nothing numerically.', 'One order gives speed, the other gives velocity.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rel-11', topic: 'relative', level: 3, q: 'Explain why all motion is described as relative.', answers: ['Any measured velocity depends on the observer’s frame — there is no frame-independent rest state to compare against.', 'Because everything in the universe is moving at the same speed.', 'Because velocity cannot be measured, only estimated from distances.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rel-12', topic: 'relative', level: 3, q: 'Two walkers move toward each other at different speeds. Each sees the other approaching at…', answers: ['the sum of their speeds, because their velocities point in opposite directions.', 'the difference of their speeds, since they move along one line.', 'a speed that depends only on the faster walker.'], correct: 0 },

  // ---------- Rotational Motion ----------
  { id: 'rot-01', topic: 'rotational', level: 1, q: 'Two temple gates have equal mass, but one is harder to start rotating. Which rotational quantity explains the difference?', answers: ['Moment of inertia, because it measures resistance to angular acceleration and depends on mass distribution.', 'Torque, because it is an object’s permanent resistance to turning.', 'Angular momentum, because every stationary gate already possesses it.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rot-02', topic: 'rotational', level: 1, q: 'An explorer pushes a Rotorwilds gate away from its hinge and produces angular acceleration. Which quantity plays the rotational role of resultant force?', answers: ['Net torque, because τ_net = Iα.', 'Linear momentum, because p = mv determines the turning effect.', 'Power, because all rotational motion requires a continuous energy supply.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rot-03', topic: 'rotational', level: 1, q: 'A guardian shifts stone counterweights farther from its axle without changing their mass. What does I = Σmr² predict?', answers: ['Its moment of inertia increases strongly because each mass contribution is weighted by r².', 'Its moment of inertia is unchanged because only total mass determines I.', 'Its moment of inertia decreases because distant masses rotate through a larger path.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rot-04', topic: 'rotational', level: 1, q: 'A carved wheel completes one revolution and sweeps an arc equal to its full circumference. How is this angular displacement expressed?', answers: ['As 2π radians, since θ = s/r = 2πr/r.', 'As π radians, because a complete revolution contains two semicircles.', 'As 360 radians, because each degree is one radian.'], correct: 0 },
  {"id":"rot-05","topic":"rotational","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-rotational","q":"Explain why moving mass outward on a wheel increases its moment of inertia.","answers":["Increasing distance from the axis increases each moved mass contribution mr².","Increasing distance from the axis increases angular speed and therefore inertia.","Increasing distance from the axis increases total mass without changing mr²."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The radius term is squared, so distance from the axis amplifies each mass’s contribution.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"rot-06","topic":"rotational","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-rotational","q":"Why does a force whose line of action passes through the axle produce no torque?","answers":["The perpendicular distance from the axle to the force's line of action is zero.","The force has no magnitude once its line of action intersects the rotation axle.","The radius must be zero whenever a force is applied to a point on a rotating object."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The lever arm is the perpendicular distance to the force's line of action. It is zero here even if the force is applied away from the axle. Zero torque does not mean zero force.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"rot-07","topic":"rotational","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-rotational","q":"Why do the constant angular acceleration equations mirror the linear ones?","answers":["Constant α links θ and ω in the same way constant a links displacement and velocity.","Constant α means constant ω, just as constant linear acceleration means constant speed.","Constant α is valid only at constant radius, so linear and angular speeds must match."],"correct":0,"explanation":"θ, ω and α are linked by the same rules as s, v and a, so the same algebra produces the same equations.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  { id: 'rot-08', topic: 'rotational', level: 2, q: 'Why is ω = 2πf for steady rotation?', answers: ['Each revolution sweeps 2π radians, so radians per second equal 2π times revolutions per second.', 'The 2π converts frequency into seconds.', 'ω counts radians, and f already includes one full turn.'], correct: 0 },
  {"id":"rot-09","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-rotational","q":"A spinning guardian pulls its arms in and spins faster. Justify this using angular momentum.","answers":["I falls; with negligible external torque, constant Iω requires a higher ω.","I falls; with negligible external torque, constant angular speed requires a lower L.","I falls; with negligible external torque, constant kinetic energy requires a higher L."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Pulling the arms in reduces I; with no external torque L = Iω is conserved, so ω must increase.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"rot-10","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-rotational","q":"Why does angular momentum conservation require zero net external torque, not just zero net force?","answers":["A force couple can have zero net force but a nonzero torque that changes L.","Zero net force always gives zero torque because equal forces cancel their moments.","A nonzero torque changes only rotational energy, leaving angular momentum unchanged."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Equal opposite forces at different lines of action form a couple: zero resultant force but nonzero torque. Angular momentum changes under external torque, while linear momentum changes under external resultant force.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"rot-11","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-rotational","q":"Two wheels have equal mass; one has it all at the rim, the other at the hub. Discuss their rotational inertia.","answers":["The rim-loaded wheel has greater I because more mass is farther from the axis.","The hub-loaded wheel has greater I because more mass is concentrated near the axis.","The wheels have equal I because equal mass makes its radial distribution irrelevant."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The rim wheel has the larger I, because its mass sits at larger radius and r is squared in Σmr².","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"rot-12","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-rotational","q":"A rigid wheel of fixed rotational inertia spins with zero net external torque. What happens to its angular velocity?","answers":["With fixed I and zero external torque, angular momentum and angular velocity stay fixed.","With fixed I and zero external torque, angular speed steadily falls toward zero.","With fixed I and zero external torque, angular speed rises as angular momentum falls."],"correct":0,"explanation":"With constant I and zero net external torque, L = Iω is conserved. Hence ω remains constant; this conclusion would need revisiting if mass distribution changed.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},

  // ---------- Rolling Motion ----------
  { id: 'rol-01', topic: 'rolling', level: 1, q: 'A stone wheel crosses dry ground without leaving a skid mark. Which relationship must hold between its centre-of-mass speed and angular speed?', answers: ['v_COM = rω, so the contact point has zero velocity relative to the ground.', 'v_COM = 2rω, because the upper rim travels twice as fast as the centre.', 'v_COM = ω, because linear and angular speeds are interchangeable.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rol-02', topic: 'rolling', level: 1, q: 'A rolling boulder descends into the Rotorwilds. Which energy model accounts for all of its macroscopic motion?', answers: ['K = ½mv_COM² + ½Iω², combining translation and rotation.', 'K = ½mv_COM² only, because rotation contributes no separate energy.', 'K = ½Iω² only, because the boulder is instantaneously rotating about its contact point.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rol-03', topic: 'rolling', level: 1, q: 'The centre of a rolling seed pod advances while markings on its rim circle the centre. How should its motion be modelled?', answers: ['As translation of the centre of mass combined with rotation about the centre of mass.', 'As two translations, one parallel and one perpendicular to the ground.', 'As pure rotation about one fixed point on the ground for the entire journey.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rol-04', topic: 'rolling', level: 2, q: 'Explain why the point of a rolling wheel touching the ground is instantaneously at rest.', answers: ['Its backward rotational speed rω equals the forward speed v_COM, so the two cancel at the contact point.', 'Friction freezes the contact point for an instant each turn.', 'The ground pushes the contact point backward into place.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rol-05', topic: 'rolling', level: 2, q: 'Why does the top point of a rolling wheel move at 2v_COM?', answers: ['Its rotational speed rω and the translational speed v_COM point the same way, so they add.', 'The top of the wheel is lighter, so it moves faster.', 'Gravity gives the top point an extra push downward along the arc.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rol-06', topic: 'rolling', level: 2, q: 'A wheel cannot maintain rolling without slipping when v ≠ rω. Explain why.', answers: ['The contact point then slides relative to the ground — the spin and the travel no longer match.', 'The wheel loses its moment of inertia when speeds mismatch.', 'Rolling requires the centre of mass to stay still.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rol-07', topic: 'rolling', level: 2, q: 'A boulder rolls from rest down a slope of height h. Describe its energy budget at the bottom.', answers: ['The lost mgh has split between translational kinetic energy and rotational kinetic energy.', 'All of the mgh is translational — rotation comes free.', 'The rotation was powered by friction heating the slope.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rol-08', topic: 'rolling', level: 3, q: 'A solid sphere races a hoop down the same slope, both rolling without slipping. Justify which wins.', answers: ['The sphere — its smaller moment of inertia sends less energy into rotation, leaving more for forward speed.', 'The hoop — its rim mass stores energy that releases as speed.', 'They tie, since both lose the same height and energy is conserved.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rol-09', topic: 'rolling', level: 3, q: 'Why does a smaller moment of inertia give a higher speed at the bottom of a slope?', answers: ['Less of the fixed energy budget is stored as rotational kinetic energy, so more becomes translational.', 'A small-I object is effectively lighter downhill.', 'Small-I objects grip the slope better and slip less.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rol-10', topic: 'rolling', level: 3, q: 'A rolling ball and a frictionless sliding block race down the same height. Discuss the result.', answers: ['The block arrives faster — none of its energy is diverted into rotation.', 'The ball arrives faster, because rolling adds extra energy.', 'They arrive together, because both conserve the same total energy.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rol-11', topic: 'rolling', level: 3, q: 'Static friction acts on a wheel rolling without slipping. Explain why it does no work.', answers: ['It acts at the contact point, which is instantaneously at rest — force times zero displacement is zero work.', 'Static friction is too weak to do meaningful work.', 'Rolling motion cancels friction’s energy automatically.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rol-12', topic: 'rolling', level: 3, q: 'What does ω = v/r express physically for a rolling wheel?', answers: ['The spin rate is set by how fast the wheel travels and how big it is.', 'The radius grows in proportion to the speed.', 'The angular speed is fixed once the wheel leaves the slope.'], correct: 0 },

  // ---------- Circular Motion ----------
  { id: 'cir-01', topic: 'circular', level: 1, q: 'A glowing beetle moves at constant speed around a circular temple rim. How does its velocity change during the motion?', answers: ['It remains tangent to the rim but continuously changes direction.', 'It points toward the centre and remains constant throughout the orbit.', 'It points outward, balancing the inward centripetal force.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'cir-02', topic: 'circular', level: 1, q: 'A vine supplies the inward force that swings a lantern in a circle. Why should “centripetal force” not be drawn as an additional force?', answers: ['It names the net inward result of real forces such as tension, gravity or friction.', 'It is an outward reaction force that cancels the vine tension.', 'It is a separate fundamental force created whenever a path curves.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'cir-03', topic: 'circular', level: 1, q: 'A falcon circles the temple at uniform speed. Which statement correctly distinguishes its speed, velocity and acceleration?', answers: ['Its speed is constant, while its velocity changes direction and its acceleration always points toward the centre.', 'Its velocity is constant because it returns to the same direction after every revolution.', 'Its acceleration is constant as a vector because its magnitude v²/r does not change.'], correct: 0 },
  {"id":"cir-04","topic":"circular","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-circular","q":"An object moves at constant speed in a circle. Explain why it is still accelerating.","answers":["Velocity changes direction even though its magnitude remains constant.","Velocity is constant because only its magnitude determines acceleration.","Acceleration is tangential because the object travels along the tangent."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Its direction changes continuously, and a changing velocity means acceleration exists.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  { id: 'cir-05', topic: 'circular', level: 2, q: 'A ball whirls on a string in a horizontal circle. What provides the centripetal force?', answers: ['The tension in the string, directed along it toward the centre.', 'The ball’s weight, pulling it around the loop.', 'A centrifugal push outward from the circle’s centre.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'cir-06', topic: 'circular', level: 2, q: 'A car turns on a flat roundabout. What force keeps it on the circular path?', answers: ['Friction between the tyres and the road, acting inward.', 'The engine’s thrust, aimed sideways.', 'The normal force from the road surface.'], correct: 0 },
  {"id":"cir-07","topic":"circular","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-circular","q":"Why is “centrifugal force” a mislabel in an inertial frame?","answers":["Without an inward resultant, the instantaneous velocity carries the object tangentially.","Without an inward resultant, an outward force carries the object radially away.","With an inward resultant, an equal outward force cancels it and maintains the circle."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The outward tendency is just inertia — objects not being pushed inward would go straight, not outward.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"cir-08","topic":"circular","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-circular","q":"A conical pendulum swings in a steady circle. Describe the resultant of tension and weight.","answers":["Vertical components cancel; the remaining horizontal force points toward the centre.","Horizontal components cancel; the remaining vertical force holds the bob in its orbit.","Weight and tension cancel completely because the bob remains at a constant height."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The vertical tension component balances weight, while the horizontal tension component supplies mω²r. Constant height does not mean zero resultant force in every direction.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"cir-09","topic":"circular","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-circular","q":"A stone on a taut string completes a vertical circle under gravity, without drag or driving work. Why is tension larger at the bottom than at the top?","answers":["The bottom speed is higher and tension must exceed weight to give the inward resultant.","The bottom speed is lower and tension must equal weight to keep the string length fixed.","The top speed is higher and tension must oppose weight to give the inward resultant."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Energy conservation gives a higher bottom speed. At the bottom T = mv²/r + mg; at the top T = mv²/r − mg. Both the speed change and weight's radial sign contribute.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"cir-10","topic":"circular","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-circular","q":"For a stone on a flexible string in a vertical circle, explain the limiting top speed √(gr) for the string to remain just taut.","answers":["At the limiting top speed, tension is zero and weight supplies the radial resultant.","At the limiting top speed, weight is zero and tension supplies the radial resultant.","At the limiting top speed, tension balances weight and the radial resultant is zero."],"correct":0,"explanation":"A flexible string can pull but cannot push. At the limiting top speed tension is zero, so mg = mv²/r. Below it the required tension would be negative, so the taut circular-path model fails.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"cir-11","topic":"circular","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-circular","q":"A rider at the top inside a vertical loop has speed √(gr). With gravity and an inward normal force as the only forces, why is apparent weight zero?","answers":["Normal force is zero while gravity still supplies the inward acceleration.","Gravity is zero while normal force still supplies the inward acceleration.","Both forces balance while the rider continues with zero acceleration."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The reaction force drops to zero — gravity alone provides all the centripetal force, so nothing pushes back on the rider.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"cir-12","topic":"circular","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-circular","q":"A banked curve needs no friction at its ideal speed. Justify this.","answers":["The normal force has an upward component balancing weight and an inward component.","The weight has an upward component balancing support and an inward component.","The normal force balances weight exactly, leaving a separate inward force to turn the car."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The normal force resolves so its vertical part balances weight and its inward part supplies the centripetal force.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},

  // ---------- Orbital Motion ----------
  { id: 'orb-01', topic: 'orbital', level: 1, q: 'A temple beacon circles Jamnanji without continuous thrust. Which interaction supplies the required centripetal force?', answers: ['Gravitational attraction toward Jamnanji’s centre.', 'Small tangential thrusters that continually push it forward.', 'Atmospheric pressure acting inward at orbital altitude.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'orb-02', topic: 'orbital', level: 1, q: 'Why does orbital speed not depend on the satellite’s mass?', answers: ['The satellite’s mass cancels when gravitational force is set equal to mv²/r.', 'Small satellites are too light to affect their own motion.', 'Mass is folded into the constant G.'], correct: 0 },
  {"id":"orb-03","topic":"orbital","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-orbital","q":"Explain why astronauts feel weightless while orbiting.","answers":["Both fall together, so no supporting contact force is needed on the astronaut.","Both lie beyond Earth's gravitational field, so neither needs an inward force.","The spacecraft shields gravity, so the astronaut moves without gravitational acceleration."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The station and the astronaut fall toward Earth together — in free fall, nothing pushes back on them.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"orb-04","topic":"orbital","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-orbital","q":"A satellite is in a stable circular orbit above Earth's surface. How can gravity act continuously without making it strike Earth?","answers":["Its sideways motion and inward acceleration combine into a path that misses Earth.","Its sideways motion creates an outward force that cancels the gravitational force.","Its sideways motion removes gravity once the satellite reaches the required altitude."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Gravity curves its path into a circle — it is continuously falling while also moving sideways fast enough to keep missing the planet.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"orb-05","topic":"orbital","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-orbital","q":"A satellite moves to a higher circular orbit. Explain what happens to its speed.","answers":["Speed falls because balancing gravity with mv²/r gives v² = GM/r.","Speed rises because a greater circumference requires faster travel at fixed period.","Speed stays fixed because gravity does no work during either circular orbit."],"correct":0,"explanation":"For circular orbits around the same central mass, GMm/r² = mv²/r gives v = √(GM/r). A larger radius gives lower circular speed; the transfer between orbits is a separate process.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  { id: 'orb-06', topic: 'orbital', level: 2, q: 'Why are the units of g both N kg⁻¹ and m s⁻²?', answers: ['Force per unit mass is an acceleration — the two units describe the same quantity.', 'They are two different quantities that happen to share a symbol.', 'N kg⁻¹ applies on Earth, m s⁻² only in orbit.'], correct: 0 },
  {"id":"orb-07","topic":"orbital","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-orbital","q":"Use T = 2π√(r³/GM) to justify why higher orbits have longer periods.","answers":["Larger r means both a longer path and a lower speed, so the period increases.","Larger r means a longer path but proportionally higher speed, so period stays fixed.","Larger r means a lower speed and a shorter path, so the period must decrease."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The period grows with r raised to the power 3/2, so a larger radius always means a longer period.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"orb-08","topic":"orbital","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-orbital","q":"Outside a spherical planet, how does Newton's gravitation law relate gravitational field strength to centre-to-centre distance?","answers":["For a spherical source outside its surface, g = F/m = GM/r².","For a spherical source outside its surface, g = Fm = GMm²/r².","For a spherical source outside its surface, g = GM/r because force acts radially."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Gravitational field strength is force per unit test mass. Dividing F = GMm/r² by m gives g = GM/r² outside a spherical source. The radius is measured from the planet's centre, not from its surface.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"orb-09","topic":"orbital","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-orbital","q":"Two low-mass satellites follow circular orbits of the same radius around the same planet. Why do their speeds match?","answers":["Their speeds match because satellite mass cancels from the orbital force equation.","The heavier satellite is slower because its larger inertia requires a longer period.","The heavier satellite is faster because its stronger gravitational force dominates."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Both gravitational force and inertial resistance scale with satellite mass. Cancelling m from GMm/r² = mv²/r leaves the same speed for both satellites at the same radius.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"orb-10","topic":"orbital","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-orbital","q":"Two satellites follow circular orbits at different radii around the same planet. Compare their speeds and periods.","answers":["The lower circular orbit has greater speed and a shorter period.","The lower circular orbit has greater speed and a longer period.","The lower circular orbit has lower speed but the same orbital period."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The navigation beacon moves faster and completes each orbit sooner, since orbital speed decreases while period increases with radius.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"orb-11","topic":"orbital","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-orbital","q":"Why does v = 2πr/T hold for circular orbits?","answers":["Constant orbital speed equals circumference divided by the time for one revolution.","Constant orbital speed equals radius divided by the time for one revolution.","Constant orbital speed equals circumference multiplied by the orbital period."],"correct":0,"explanation":"One complete circular orbit covers 2πr in time T. With uniform circular speed, distance divided by elapsed time gives v = 2πr/T. This is not the instantaneous-speed rule for a general ellipse.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"orb-12","topic":"orbital","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-orbital","q":"A probe is farther from the centre of the same spherical planet. How does the gravitational field strength change?","answers":["The field is weaker because its strength varies inversely with squared separation.","The field is stronger because the larger orbit encloses more gravitational field lines.","The field is unchanged because the probe mass and central mass remain unchanged."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Outside the spherical planet, g = GM/r². Greater centre-to-centre distance means a weaker field; probe mass does not appear in the field-strength expression.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},

  // ---------- Simple Harmonic Motion ----------
  { id: 'shm-01', topic: 'shm', level: 1, q: 'A crystal displaced from the centre of a temple trap experiences F = −kx. Why does this condition produce simple harmonic motion?', answers: ['The restoring force is proportional to displacement and always directed toward equilibrium.', 'Any force that repeats in time automatically produces simple harmonic motion.', 'A constant one-direction force returns the crystal at equal time intervals.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'shm-02', topic: 'shm', level: 1, q: 'A resonant seed pod obeys a = −ω²x. At which positions is the magnitude of its acceleration greatest, and why?', answers: ['At the extreme displacements, because |a| is proportional to |x|.', 'At equilibrium, because this is where its speed is greatest.', 'Halfway to each extreme, because acceleration and velocity peak together.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'shm-03', topic: 'shm', level: 1, q: 'A hanging temple lantern oscillates about a stable central position. What additional condition would make the motion simple harmonic?', answers: ['Its acceleration must be proportional to displacement and directed toward equilibrium.', 'Its path must be the projection of a real object moving in a circle.', 'Its motion must eventually stop because of resistive forces.'], correct: 0 },
  {"id":"shm-04","topic":"shm","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-shm","q":"Why is speed maximum at equilibrium in an ideal horizontal mass–spring oscillator?","answers":["Potential energy is least there, so conserved mechanical energy gives maximum kinetic energy.","Restoring force is greatest there, so the oscillator reaches its maximum acceleration and speed.","Potential energy is greatest there, so it supplies the oscillator's greatest instantaneous speed."],"correct":0,"explanation":"At equilibrium spring potential energy is minimum. With total mechanical energy fixed, kinetic energy is maximum. Zero resultant force at that instant does not mean zero velocity.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  { id: 'shm-05', topic: 'shm', level: 2, q: 'At the extremes of an oscillation, the energy of an ideal oscillator is…', answers: ['entirely potential — the mass is momentarily still.', 'entirely kinetic — the turning points move fastest.', 'half kinetic and half potential, on average.'], correct: 0 },
  {"id":"shm-06","topic":"shm","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-shm","q":"Why is there a minus sign in a = −ω²x?","answers":["Acceleration points toward equilibrium, opposite to the displacement direction.","Acceleration always points opposite to velocity, slowing the oscillator continuously.","Acceleration points away from equilibrium whenever the displacement is positive."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The acceleration always opposes the displacement, pointing back toward equilibrium.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"shm-07","topic":"shm","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-shm","q":"A greater mass oscillates on the same ideal spring. Why does the period increase?","answers":["Greater inertia lowers ω = √(k/m), so the period increases.","Greater weight increases k automatically, so the period stays unchanged.","Greater inertia raises ω = √(k/m), so the period decreases."],"correct":0,"explanation":"For an ideal spring, ω² = k/m. Greater mass reduces restoring acceleration for a given displacement, lowering frequency and increasing T = 2π√(m/k).","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"shm-08","topic":"shm","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-shm","q":"Why does a lightly damped oscillator have a large response when driven near its natural frequency?","answers":["Driving near the natural frequency transfers energy efficiently, producing a large response.","Driving at any frequency transfers identical energy each cycle, producing the same response.","Driving near the natural frequency removes the restoring force, producing steady translation."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Near resonance the driver transfers energy efficiently. With damping, steady amplitude is set by input balancing losses. The displacement-amplitude peak need not be exactly at the undamped natural frequency.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"shm-09","topic":"shm","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-shm","q":"What does damping do to an oscillation?","answers":["Dissipation reduces mechanical energy, so the oscillation amplitude decays.","Dissipation reduces displacement but leaves total mechanical energy unchanged.","Dissipation removes mass each cycle, so the oscillation amplitude decreases."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Damping transfers mechanical energy to other forms, usually heat. Successive oscillation amplitudes fall; total energy including the surroundings is still conserved.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"shm-10","topic":"shm","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-shm","q":"Discuss the energy changes over one full oscillation of an ideal mass–spring system.","answers":["Kinetic and potential energies exchange while their sum remains constant.","Kinetic and potential energies peak together while their sum remains constant.","Kinetic energy vanishes at equilibrium while potential energy vanishes at the ends."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Energy swaps between potential and kinetic twice per cycle, while the total stays constant.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"shm-11","topic":"shm","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-shm","q":"A simple pendulum has the same length and small release angle, but a heavier bob. Explain why its period is unchanged.","answers":["Weight and inertia scale together, leaving the small-angle period independent of mass.","Greater weight gives a proportionally shorter period while inertia has no effect.","Greater inertia gives a proportionally longer period while weight has no effect."],"correct":0,"explanation":"None — the mass cancels out, leaving T = 2π√(l/g) dependent only on length and g.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"shm-12","topic":"shm","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-shm","q":"A damped oscillator is driven sinusoidally at a frequency below its natural frequency. After transients have decayed, what frequency does it follow?","answers":["After transients fade, its motion follows the driving frequency rather than its natural one.","After transients fade, its motion follows only its natural frequency rather than the driver's.","After transients fade, its motion follows the sum of the natural and driving frequencies."],"correct":0,"explanation":"In sinusoidal steady state a linear damped oscillator follows the driving frequency. The natural frequency affects response amplitude and phase, but does not replace the driver's frequency.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},

  // ---------- Momentum and Impulse (extended) ----------
  {"id":"mom-13","topic":"momentum","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-momentum","q":"Two stopping-force graphs have equal areas. What can be concluded about impulse and peak force without assuming their shapes?","answers":["Equal graph areas mean equal impulses; duration alone does not fix the peak force.","Equal graph areas mean equal peak forces; duration determines only the average impulse.","Equal graph durations mean equal impulses; only the peak force controls momentum change."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Graph area gives impulse. Equal areas do not determine the peak or duration separately. A longer stopping time lowers average force for a fixed impulse but does not alone prove a lower peak.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"mom-14","topic":"momentum","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-momentum","q":"Two mud-covered boulders collide and stick. For their fixed initial momenta, why does this outcome represent the greatest possible kinetic-energy loss?","answers":["Sticking eliminates relative motion, minimising kinetic energy for the fixed total momentum.","Sticking eliminates total momentum, minimising kinetic energy regardless of external forces.","Sticking preserves both initial kinetic energy and momentum by giving the masses equal speed."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Momentum still fixes their shared final velocity, while sticking removes all relative motion between them and transfers the maximum permitted kinetic energy to other forms.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"mom-15","topic":"momentum","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-momentum","q":"A seed pod strikes a temple wall and rebounds. Which vector method correctly determines its change in momentum?","answers":["Subtract initial momentum vectorially from final momentum, preserving both directions.","Subtract initial speed from final speed, then assign the result the final direction.","Add the magnitudes of the momenta regardless of the angle between their directions."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Use Δp = p_f − p_i, equivalently adding the reversed initial momentum vector to the final vector.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},

  // ---------- Energy and Work (extended) ----------
  { id: 'ene-13', topic: 'energy', level: 1, q: 'A vine does positive work while accelerating a supply sled. Which energy statement is equivalent to this description?', answers: ['The net work equals the sled’s change in energy: W = ΔE.', 'Work is the rate at which the sled’s energy changes, so W = ΔE/Δt.', 'Work is the impulse delivered to the sled, so W = FΔt.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ene-14', topic: 'energy', level: 3, q: 'A vine cart enters the bottom of a vertical loop and reaches the top without resistive losses. Justify using energy rather than a constant-acceleration model to relate its two speeds.', answers: ['Its height and resultant force change around the loop, while conservation of mechanical energy links the endpoint speeds directly through the 2r height gain.', 'Circular motion automatically keeps its speed constant, so no force analysis is required.', 'The resultant force is constant around the loop, making the constant-acceleration equations equivalent to energy conservation.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ene-15', topic: 'energy', level: 2, q: 'Two springs are stretched by the same amount. Discuss which stores more elastic energy.', answers: ['The stiffer one — a larger spring constant k gives more energy at the same extension.', 'The softer one, because it stretches more willingly.', 'Both store the same, since the extensions match.'], correct: 0 },

  // ---------- Centre of Mass (extended) ----------
  {"id":"com-13","topic":"com","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-com","q":"In a_COM = Σmᵢaᵢ/Σmᵢ, what does the averaging mean physically?","answers":["Each acceleration vector is weighted by mass before the components are averaged.","Each acceleration magnitude is averaged equally before assigning the largest direction.","Each force is weighted by speed before the total is divided by the combined mass."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Acceleration is a vector, so take mass-weighted component averages. Equal accelerations of unequal masses contribute unequally; averaging magnitudes can lose essential direction information.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  { id: 'com-14', topic: 'com', level: 1, q: 'A tumbling relic follows a projectile path while rotating rapidly. Why does a centre-of-mass model simplify the analysis?', answers: ['It allows translation of the centre of mass and rotation about it to be treated separately.', 'It removes the need to consider external forces such as gravity.', 'It transforms to a frame in which the system’s total momentum is always zero.'], correct: 0 },

  // ---------- Rotational Motion (extended) ----------
  { id: 'rot-13', topic: 'rotational', level: 1, q: 'A freely spinning jungle mechanism preserves a quantity when no external torque acts. Which quantity is conserved, and how is it expressed for a rigid body?', answers: ['Angular momentum, L = Iω.', 'Torque, τ = Fr, because the turning effect remains stored.', 'Moment of inertia, I = mr², because it measures the mechanism’s motion.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rot-14', topic: 'rotational', level: 2, q: 'Tangential acceleration a_t = rα tells you that…', answers: ['points farther from the axis accelerate more for the same angular acceleration.', 'every point on the wheel shares one acceleration value.', 'angular acceleration grows with distance from the axis.'], correct: 0 },
  {"id":"rot-15","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-rotational","q":"Justify why L = mvr for a point mass moving in a circle.","answers":["For a circular point mass, mr² multiplied by v/r gives mvr about the centre.","For a circular point mass, mr multiplied by v/r gives mv about the centre.","For a circular point mass, mr² multiplied by vr gives mvr³ about the centre."],"correct":0,"explanation":"For a point mass at radius r, I = mr² and ω = v/r. Hence L = Iω = mvr about the circle centre. For general motion use perpendicular distance to the momentum's line of action.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"rot-16","topic":"rotational","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-rotational","q":"Why is τ_net = Iα called Newton’s second law for rotation?","answers":["Net torque sets angular acceleration, with I describing resistance to that acceleration.","Net torque sets angular velocity, with I describing resistance to any ongoing rotation.","Net torque sets angular displacement, with I describing how quickly rotation stops."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Net torque changes rotational motion while I resists that change — the exact role F and m play linearly.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},

  // ---------- Circular Motion (extended) ----------
  { id: 'cir-13', topic: 'circular', level: 1, q: 'Two parrots follow circular paths; one maintains constant speed while the other speeds up. What additional acceleration does the second parrot require?', answers: ['A tangential component that changes its speed, as well as the radial component that changes direction.', 'No acceleration, because both paths remain circular.', 'An outward component that turns sections of its path into straight lines.'], correct: 0 },
  {"id":"cir-14","topic":"circular","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-circular","q":"A stone on a taut string completes a vertical circle under gravity with no losses or driving work. Compare its top and bottom kinetic energies.","answers":["Bottom kinetic energy exceeds top kinetic energy by the gravitational energy lost.","Bottom kinetic energy equals top kinetic energy because string tension does no work.","Top kinetic energy exceeds bottom kinetic energy because gravity resists downward motion."],"correct":0,"explanation":"½mv²_top + mg(2r) = ½mv²_bottom — the height difference 2r accounts for the speed change.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"cir-15","topic":"circular","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-circular","q":"A car maintains a circular path on a bank at less than the frictionless design speed. In which direction must static friction act along the slope?","answers":["Static friction must act up the bank to prevent sliding inward down the slope.","Static friction must act down the bank to prevent sliding outward up the slope.","Static friction must vanish because the normal force always sets the required inward force."],"correct":0,"explanation":"It tends to slide down the slope, so friction acts up the slope to help hold the line.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},

  // ---------- Orbital Motion (extended) ----------
  {"id":"orb-13","topic":"orbital","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-orbital","q":"The same satellite orbits a heavier planet at the same radius. Discuss its new orbital speed.","answers":["Greater central mass requires a greater orbital speed at the same radius.","Greater central mass requires a lower orbital speed at the same radius.","Greater central mass leaves orbital speed fixed because satellite mass cancels."],"correct":0,"explanation":"At fixed circular orbital radius, v = √(GM/r) increases with the central mass M. Satellite mass cancels, but central mass does not.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"orb-14","topic":"orbital","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-orbital","q":"Why do we set F_g = F_c to derive the orbital speed?","answers":["Gravity is the actual inward force; mv²/r is the required inward resultant.","Gravity is balanced by mv²/r, leaving no resultant force on the satellite.","Gravity supplies tangential force while mv²/r supplies a separate radial force."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Gravity is the force doing the centripetal job, so both expressions describe one and the same force.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"orb-15","topic":"orbital","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-orbital","q":"From T = 2π√(r³/GM), what relationship between period and radius follows?","answers":["Squaring the period relationship gives T² proportional to r³ for fixed M.","Squaring the period relationship gives T proportional to r³ for fixed M.","Squaring the period relationship gives T² proportional to r for fixed M."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Squaring gives T² = 4π²r³/(GM). For satellites around the same central mass the coefficient is constant, so T² is proportional to r³, not T proportional to r³.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},

  // ---------- Simple Harmonic Motion (extended) ----------
  { id: 'shm-13', topic: 'shm', level: 1, q: 'A Resonant Heart moves between x = −0.30 m and x = +0.30 m about equilibrium. What is its amplitude, and why?', answers: ['0.30 m, because amplitude is the maximum displacement from equilibrium.', '0.60 m, because amplitude is the full distance between turning points.', '1.20 m, because amplitude is the distance travelled in a complete cycle.'], correct: 0 },
  {"id":"shm-14","topic":"shm","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-shm","q":"A simple pendulum is made longer while g and its small angular amplitude stay fixed. Explain the change in period.","answers":["Greater length reduces restoring acceleration per displacement, increasing period.","Greater length increases restoring acceleration per displacement, decreasing period.","Greater length increases distance and speed proportionally, leaving period unchanged."],"correct":0,"explanation":"For small angles the restoring tangential acceleration per displacement is g/l. Increasing l reduces this coefficient, lowering angular frequency and increasing T = 2π√(l/g).","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  { id: 'shm-15', topic: 'shm', level: 1, q: 'Temple drums apply repeated pushes to a hanging bridge. What distinguishes the resulting forced oscillation from a free oscillation?', answers: ['An external periodic force drives the bridge and sets the steady-state oscillation frequency.', 'Friction alone supplies the restoring force and fixes the bridge’s frequency.', 'The bridge oscillates only through its own restoring force, with no external energy input.'], correct: 0 },
  {"id":"shm-16","topic":"shm","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-shm","q":"A motion repeats about a mean position. Why is that information insufficient to establish simple harmonic motion?","answers":["Periodic motion need not be SHM; the restoring acceleration must also be proportional to −x.","Periodic motion must be SHM; repeating the path guarantees acceleration proportional to −x.","Periodic motion must have constant speed; otherwise it cannot repeat the same motion."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Repetition alone does not establish the SHM force law. Ideal SHM requires acceleration proportional to displacement and directed toward equilibrium: a = −ω²x with constant ω.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},

  // ---------- Momentum and Impulse (expedition set) ----------
  {"id":"mom-16","topic":"momentum","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-momentum","q":"A light canoe and a heavily loaded raft receive equal impulses from stretched vines. Compare their resulting momentum changes and velocity changes.","answers":["Equal vector impulses give equal Δp, but the lighter object has larger Δv.","Equal vector impulses give equal Δv, but the heavier object has larger Δp.","Equal vector impulses give equal kinetic energy gains regardless of initial velocity."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Their momentum changes are equal, but the lighter canoe has the larger velocity change because Δv = Δp/m.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"mom-17","topic":"momentum","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-momentum","q":"An explorer jumps forward from a stationary raft. Discuss the motion of the explorer–raft system while horizontal external forces are negligible.","answers":["Opposite momenta keep the combined centre at rest; the heavier raft recoils more slowly.","Opposite velocities keep the combined centre at rest; mass does not affect recoil speed.","Opposite forces cancel on each object separately; neither can gain momentum during the jump."],"correct":0,"explanation":"They gain equal and opposite momenta, so the raft recoils more slowly if it is more massive and the system’s centre-of-mass velocity remains zero.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"mom-18","topic":"momentum","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-momentum","q":"On a frictionless horizontal surface, a moving object strikes a stationary object and they stick. Why is their common speed lower and in the original direction?","answers":["The conserved eastward momentum is shared by a larger mass, reducing the common speed.","The conserved eastward kinetic energy is shared by a larger mass, preserving common speed.","The conserved eastward force is shared by a larger mass, reducing the final total momentum."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Eastward momentum is conserved but is shared by a greater combined mass, so the common velocity must be smaller.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"mom-19","topic":"momentum","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-momentum","q":"A force–time graph for a rhino striking a gate has a sharp peak. A padded gate produces the same graph area with a lower peak. What can be concluded?","answers":["Impulse is unchanged; the lower graph maximum means a lower peak force.","Impulse is reduced; the lower graph maximum means a smaller momentum change.","Impulse is unchanged; the lower graph maximum guarantees the same collision duration."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Equal force–time areas mean equal impulse. A lower maximum ordinate means a lower peak force. These facts do not by themselves specify the detailed graph shape or duration.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"mom-20","topic":"momentum","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-momentum","q":"Two carts collide while a small external friction force acts. When is treating their momentum as conserved a defensible approximation?","answers":["External impulse is negligible compared with the momenta being exchanged in the collision.","External force is smaller than either object's weight, regardless of how long it acts.","External energy loss is small, which by itself ensures momentum is exactly conserved."],"correct":0,"explanation":"When the collision time is so short that the external friction impulse is negligible compared with the carts’ exchanged impulses.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},

  // ---------- Energy and Work (expedition set) ----------
  { id: 'ene-16', topic: 'energy', level: 2, q: 'Two routes climb to the same canopy platform: one is steep and short, the other gentle and long. Ignoring friction, compare the work done against gravity.', answers: ['The work is the same on both routes because it depends on the vertical height gain mgΔh, not the path.', 'The steep route requires more work because its force component along the path is larger.', 'The long route requires more work because work always increases with total distance travelled.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ene-17', topic: 'energy', level: 3, q: 'A vine catapult launches two equal stones with the same speed, one vertically and one at an angle. Ignoring drag, compare their maximum increases in gravitational potential energy.', answers: ['The vertical launch gains more because all its initial kinetic energy can become gravitational potential energy, while the angled stone retains horizontal kinetic energy.', 'They gain the same because their initial kinetic energies are equal and all kinetic energy vanishes at maximum height.', 'The angled launch gains more because its longer curved path lets gravity perform more negative work.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ene-18', topic: 'energy', level: 3, q: 'A supply sled descends a rough jungle slope at constant speed. Discuss the energy transfers during the descent.', answers: ['Its kinetic energy is unchanged; the lost gravitational potential energy is transferred mainly to thermal energy by friction.', 'Its gravitational potential energy becomes kinetic energy, but friction removes the extra speed afterward.', 'No energy transfer occurs because constant speed means the resultant force and all work are zero.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ene-19', topic: 'energy', level: 2, q: 'A temple winch lifts the same relic through the same height, first slowly and then quickly. Compare the work and average power, ignoring losses.', answers: ['The work is the same, but the quicker lift has greater average power because the same energy transfer occurs in less time.', 'The quicker lift requires more work because greater power always means more energy is transferred.', 'Both work and power are the same because the relic’s mass and height are unchanged.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ene-20', topic: 'energy', level: 3, q: 'A compressed spring launches a cart up a rough track, where it stops below the height predicted for a frictionless track. Account for the full energy change.', answers: ['Initial elastic energy becomes gravitational potential energy plus thermal energy, with zero kinetic energy at the turning point.', 'The missing height shows that some energy was destroyed by friction before the cart stopped.', 'All elastic energy becomes gravitational potential energy; friction changes only the time taken to climb.'], correct: 0 },

  // ---------- Centre of Mass (expedition set) ----------
  {"id":"com-15","topic":"com","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-com","q":"An explorer walks along an initially stationary freely floating raft, with negligible horizontal external force. Why does the raft move backward?","answers":["Internal motion redistributes positions while the initially resting system centre stays fixed.","Internal motion transfers the system centre to the explorer because the explorer walks forward.","Internal motion makes the raft's speed equal to the explorer's speed regardless of their masses."],"correct":0,"explanation":"With negligible horizontal external force, the system’s centre of mass must remain fixed, so the raft shifts opposite the explorer.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"com-16","topic":"com","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-com","q":"A vine bridge holds two supply crates at different positions. One crate is twice as massive as the other. Justify where the pair’s centre of mass lies.","answers":["The heavier crate is closer to the centre, with mass times distance balancing on each side.","The heavier crate is farther from the centre, with each mass divided by distance balancing.","Both crates are equally far from the centre because the support structure connects them."],"correct":0,"explanation":"It lies between them but closer to the heavier crate, with distances from the centre of mass inversely proportional to their masses.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"com-17","topic":"com","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-com","q":"A mid-air seed pod splits into two fragments under an internal spring force. Compare the fragments’ paths with the path of their combined centre of mass.","answers":["Individual paths change, but their mass-weighted position follows the original trajectory.","Individual paths change, so their unweighted midpoint always follows the original trajectory.","Individual paths change, but the centre of mass must follow the fragment with greatest speed."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The fragments follow separate paths, but their mass-weighted centre continues the projectile trajectory set by gravity.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"com-18","topic":"com","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-com","q":"An uneven idol stands on a narrow pedestal. Which condition determines whether it topples after being nudged?","answers":["Beyond the support edge, gravity produces an outward turning moment about that edge.","Beyond the support edge, gravity vanishes at the centre and shifts to the point of contact.","Beyond the support edge, mass increases on one side until the normal force becomes zero."],"correct":0,"explanation":"It topples when the vertical line through its centre of mass falls outside its base of support, producing an unbalanced torque.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"com-19","topic":"com","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-com","q":"A canoe gains rainwater concentrated at its bow. What can be concluded about its centre of mass without further hull or buoyancy information?","answers":["Adding mass at the bow shifts the centre toward the added mass; stability needs more information.","Adding mass at the bow leaves the centre fixed because water has no preferred direction.","Adding mass at the bow shifts the centre aft because the original mass opposes redistribution."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The added bow mass shifts the combined centre of mass toward the bow. Whether stability improves or worsens depends on the hull, buoyancy, vertical mass distribution and water motion; centre shift alone is insufficient.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},

  // ---------- Relative Velocity (expedition set) ----------
  { id: 'rel-13', topic: 'relative', level: 2, q: 'A canoe points directly across a river but the current carries it downstream. Explain the canoe’s ground velocity.', answers: ['It is the vector sum of the canoe’s velocity relative to the water and the water’s velocity relative to the bank.', 'It equals the canoe’s across-river speed because perpendicular current cannot affect its path.', 'It is the difference of the two speed magnitudes, directed toward the faster motion.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rel-14', topic: 'relative', level: 3, q: 'An explorer wants to land directly opposite on a flowing river. Justify why the canoe must point upstream rather than straight across.', answers: ['Its upstream velocity component relative to the water must cancel the downstream current, leaving a resultant velocity directly across.', 'Pointing upstream increases the river current until the two speeds become equal.', 'The canoe must aim upstream because relative velocities are always opposite to ground velocities.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rel-15', topic: 'relative', level: 2, q: 'Two parrots fly east at 12 m/s and 8 m/s relative to the ground. Describe the slower parrot’s velocity in the faster parrot’s frame.', answers: ['It is 4 m/s west because v_slow,fast = 8 − 12 = −4 m/s.', 'It is 20 m/s east because velocities in the same direction must be added.', 'It is stationary because both velocities point east.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rel-16', topic: 'relative', level: 3, q: 'Rain falls vertically in the ground frame but strikes the front of a moving jungle rover. Use relative velocity to explain the observation.', answers: ['Subtracting the rover’s forward velocity gives the rain a backward horizontal component in the rover frame, so its relative velocity is slanted.', 'The rover’s motion exerts a horizontal force that bends each raindrop before impact.', 'The rain remains vertical in every inertial frame because gravity fixes an absolute direction of motion.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rel-17', topic: 'relative', level: 3, q: 'A raft drifts downstream while an explorer walks upstream across its deck. Under what condition is the explorer stationary relative to the bank?', answers: ['When the explorer’s upstream velocity relative to the raft equals the raft’s downstream velocity relative to the bank.', 'When the explorer and raft have equal speeds in the same downstream direction.', 'Only when both the explorer and raft are stationary relative to the water.'], correct: 0 },

  // ---------- Rotational Motion (expedition set) ----------
  {"id":"rot-17","topic":"rotational","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-rotational","q":"Two equal perpendicular forces act on a door at different distances from the hinge. Which produces more torque?","answers":["For perpendicular pushes, the larger distance from the hinge gives greater torque.","For perpendicular pushes, equal force gives equal torque regardless of hinge distance.","For perpendicular pushes, the smaller distance gives greater torque through leverage."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The outer push produces more torque because the perpendicular lever arm is larger.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"rot-18","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-rotational","q":"A rotating platform contracts inward while no external torque acts. Discuss both angular momentum and rotational kinetic energy.","answers":["L stays fixed, ω rises, and work during contraction can raise rotational kinetic energy.","L stays fixed, ω rises, and rotational kinetic energy must stay fixed without external torque.","L rises, ω rises, and contraction supplies angular momentum without any external torque."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Angular momentum remains constant, angular speed increases as I decreases, and kinetic energy can increase through work done during contraction.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"rot-19","topic":"rotational","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-rotational","q":"A light vine is wound around two axles of different radii. The same tangential force is applied to each. Which axle has the greater angular acceleration if their moments of inertia are equal?","answers":["The larger radius gives greater torque and therefore greater α for the same I.","The larger radius gives greater inertia and therefore smaller α despite the same I.","The larger radius gives equal torque but greater α because the force travels farther."],"correct":0,"explanation":"A tangential force gives τ = rF. With equal force and equal rotational inertia, the larger application radius gives greater torque and angular acceleration α = τ/I.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"rot-20","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-rotational","q":"A flywheel and a solid disk have equal mass and radius. The flywheel has more mass near its rim. Compare their response to the same net torque.","answers":["More mass near the rim increases I and decreases α under the same torque.","More mass near the rim decreases I and increases α under the same torque.","More mass near the rim increases torque automatically and leaves α unchanged."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The flywheel has greater moment of inertia and therefore smaller angular acceleration from α = τ/I.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"rot-21","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-rotational","q":"A person on a freely rotating platform throws a held stone tangentially forward, transferring angular momentum to it. With negligible external torque, what happens to the angular momentum of the person and platform?","answers":["The throw transfers angular momentum to the stone, reducing the platform's angular momentum.","The throw transfers linear momentum only, leaving the platform's angular momentum unchanged.","The throw removes mass, so the platform must speed up regardless of the stone's launch speed."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The person/platform and stone exchange angular momentum. The stone's gain is balanced by their loss about the same axis. This alone does not determine angular speed unless the change in rotational inertia is also known.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},

  // ---------- Rolling Motion (expedition set) ----------
  { id: 'rol-13', topic: 'rolling', level: 2, q: 'A rolling wheel suddenly reaches slick mud and begins to slide while keeping the same angular speed. What relationship has failed?', answers: ['v_COM = rω no longer holds, so the contact point moves relative to the ground.', 'v_COM = 2rω no longer holds, so the top of the wheel becomes stationary.', 'The wheel has lost all rotational kinetic energy because friction is absent.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rol-14', topic: 'rolling', level: 3, q: 'A hoop and a solid cylinder roll from rest down the same slope without slipping. Use their mass distributions to compare their accelerations.', answers: ['The solid cylinder accelerates faster because its smaller I/(mr²) leaves a larger fraction of mgh for translational motion.', 'The hoop accelerates faster because its larger moment of inertia stores more energy and pulls it downhill.', 'They accelerate equally because mass, radius and vertical energy loss are identical.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rol-15', topic: 'rolling', level: 3, q: 'A driven cart wheel rolls without slipping while speeding up on level ground. Explain the role of static friction.', answers: ['Static friction supplies the horizontal interaction needed to coordinate translation and rotation, while the contact point remains instantaneously at rest.', 'Static friction must do negative work and reduce the cart’s mechanical energy whenever the wheel speeds up.', 'No friction acts during rolling without slipping because the contact point has zero velocity.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rol-16', topic: 'rolling', level: 2, q: 'A boulder rolls onto level ground with v_COM = rω. What is the speed of its highest point relative to the ground?', answers: ['2v_COM, because translational and rotational velocities add at the top.', 'v_COM, because every point on a rigid body shares the centre-of-mass speed.', 'Zero, because pure rolling makes every rim point instantaneously stationary.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'rol-17', topic: 'rolling', level: 3, q: 'A rolling object and a sliding object start with equal total kinetic energy and climb lossless tracks; the rolling track supplies enough static friction to prevent slipping. Compare their maximum heights.', answers: ['They reach the same height because each converts the same total kinetic energy into gravitational potential energy, while static friction coordinates the rolling motion without dissipating energy.', 'The rolling object reaches lower because rotational kinetic energy cannot be converted into gravitational potential energy.', 'The sliding object reaches lower because it lacks rotational energy in addition to translational energy.'], correct: 0 },

  // ---------- Circular Motion (expedition set) ----------
  {"id":"cir-16","topic":"circular","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-circular","q":"A lantern moves faster on a circular path of unchanged radius. With its mass fixed, how must the inward resultant change?","answers":["At fixed mass and radius, greater speed requires a larger inward resultant through mv²/r.","At fixed mass and radius, greater speed requires the same resultant because speed is constant.","At fixed mass and radius, greater speed requires a larger outward resultant to maintain the path."],"correct":0,"explanation":"For fixed mass and radius, the required inward resultant is mv²/r. Greater speed therefore requires a larger inward force; centripetal force is not an additional outward interaction.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"cir-17","topic":"circular","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-circular","q":"A cart remains in contact with the crest of a circular hill. Why does increasing its speed reduce the passengers' apparent weight?","answers":["Greater speed needs a larger downward resultant, leaving a smaller upward normal force.","Greater speed reduces gravitational weight, leaving the same upward normal force as before.","Greater speed needs a larger upward resultant, requiring the normal force to exceed weight."],"correct":0,"explanation":"A larger downward centripetal acceleration is required, so gravity must exceed the upward normal force by a greater amount and the normal force decreases.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"cir-18","topic":"circular","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-circular","q":"A conical pendulum of fixed length rotates steadily at a larger angular speed. How does its angle from vertical change?","answers":["Greater angular speed requires a larger angle because cosθ = g/(ω²l).","Greater angular speed requires a smaller angle because cosθ = g/(ω²l).","Greater angular speed leaves angle fixed because vertical tension must still balance weight."],"correct":0,"explanation":"For a nonzero cone angle, r = l sinθ and T sinθ = mω²r give T = mω²l. Together with T cosθ = mg this gives cosθ = g/(ω²l), so increasing ω increases θ.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"cir-19","topic":"circular","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-circular","q":"Mud reduces the available friction on a flat circular track. What happens to the maximum safe speed at the same radius?","answers":["Less available friction limits the inward resultant, so maximum safe speed decreases.","Less available friction reduces outward force, so maximum safe speed increases.","Less available friction changes tangential acceleration only, so safe turning speed is fixed."],"correct":0,"explanation":"It decreases because friction supplies the centripetal force and v_max = √(μgr).","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"cir-20","topic":"circular","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-circular","q":"A stone is released when the vine holding it in horizontal circular motion snaps. Describe and justify its immediate path in the ground frame.","answers":["Its initial velocity is tangent to the circle; gravity then curves the free-flight path downward.","Its initial velocity is radially outward; gravity then curves the free-flight path downward.","Its initial velocity is tangent, and the former tension keeps curving its path inward."],"correct":0,"explanation":"It moves along the tangent with its instantaneous velocity, then curves downward under gravity because the inward tension has vanished.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},

  // ---------- Orbital Motion (expedition set) ----------
  {"id":"orb-16","topic":"orbital","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-orbital","q":"A satellite in circular orbit receives a modest forward impulse, remaining bound to the planet. Why is it not immediately in a higher circular orbit?","answers":["A modest forward impulse gives an elliptical path; a second impulse is needed to circularise higher.","A modest forward impulse puts it directly in a higher circle with unchanged orbital speed.","A modest forward impulse reduces orbital energy, making every subsequent point lower."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Its position is unchanged at the impulse but its speed rises above circular speed there. The new bound path is an ellipse; reaching a higher circular orbit generally requires a further change in velocity.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"orb-17","topic":"orbital","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-orbital","q":"Two satellites follow circular orbits at different radii around the same planet. Which comparison describes the outer orbit?","answers":["The outer circular orbit is slower and has a longer period around the same central mass.","The outer circular orbit is faster and has a longer period around the same central mass.","The outer circular orbit is slower but has the same period around the same central mass."],"correct":0,"explanation":"For circular orbits v = √(GM/r), while T = 2π√(r³/GM). Increasing radius therefore lowers speed and lengthens period. This compares different circles, not speeds during a transfer impulse.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"orb-18","topic":"orbital","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-orbital","q":"An orbiting explorer feels weightless even though gravity is strong. Distinguish weightlessness from the absence of gravity.","answers":["Gravity remains nonzero, but apparent weight vanishes when no support force acts.","Gravity becomes zero, but apparent weight remains nonzero because the astronaut has mass.","Gravity is cancelled by normal force, so both resultant force and apparent weight vanish."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Gravity accelerates both explorer and craft together, so no supporting normal force acts and apparent weight is zero.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"orb-19","topic":"orbital","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-orbital","q":"Two spherical planets have the same radius but different masses. Which has the stronger gravitational field at its surface?","answers":["Greater central mass gives a stronger surface field at the same radius.","Greater central mass gives a weaker surface field at the same radius.","Greater central mass leaves surface field fixed because test mass cancels."],"correct":0,"explanation":"At the same radius, g = GM/r² increases with central mass. Cancellation of the test mass does not cancel the planet's mass.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"orb-20","topic":"orbital","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-orbital","q":"A satellite's tangential speed in circular orbit is slightly reduced without changing its position or direction. Describe the subsequent bound path, ignoring atmosphere.","answers":["It moves onto an ellipse with the slowing point at apocentre, unless it intersects the planet.","It moves directly onto a smaller circular orbit while keeping its original position.","It moves onto an ellipse with the slowing point at pericentre, then initially moves outward."],"correct":0,"explanation":"At that position its speed is now below circular speed, so it begins falling inward along an ellipse with this point at apocentre. If the path intersects the planet it impacts rather than completing an orbit.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},

  // ---------- Simple Harmonic Motion (expedition set) ----------
  {"id":"shm-17","topic":"shm","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-shm","q":"An ideal horizontal spring oscillator is released with a larger amplitude. Compare its total energy and period.","answers":["Greater amplitude raises total energy but leaves the ideal oscillator period unchanged.","Greater amplitude raises total energy and period because the oscillator travels farther.","Greater amplitude leaves total energy fixed but raises period because maximum speed is fixed."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The total energy ½kA² increases, but the ideal period 2π√(m/k) does not depend on amplitude. The larger travel distance is accompanied by greater speeds.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"shm-18","topic":"shm","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-shm","q":"A resonant bridge is driven close to its natural frequency. Explain why damping limits the amplitude.","answers":["Steady amplitude occurs when energy input each cycle balances dissipative loss.","Steady amplitude occurs when damping removes all restoring force from the bridge.","Steady amplitude occurs when driving stops transferring energy into the bridge."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Resistive forces dissipate energy each cycle, so the steady amplitude occurs when energy supplied by the driver balances energy lost.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"shm-19","topic":"shm","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-shm","q":"For an ideal horizontal spring oscillator of amplitude A, which symbolic argument locates equal kinetic and elastic potential energies?","answers":["Equal energies require x² = A²/2, since potential energy depends on squared displacement.","Equal energies require |x| = A/2, since potential energy depends directly on displacement.","Equal energies occur at equilibrium, since kinetic and potential energies both peak there."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The total energy is ½kA² and elastic energy is ½kx². Equal kinetic and elastic energies require ½kx² = ½(½kA²), giving x² = A²/2 and two symmetric positions.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"shm-20","topic":"shm","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-shm","q":"A mass–spring trap is taken to a jungle world with weaker gravity but remains horizontal. Discuss its period and equilibrium position.","answers":["Its ideal horizontal period and equilibrium stay unchanged because gravity has no axial component.","Its ideal horizontal period increases while equilibrium shifts because weight decreases.","Its ideal horizontal period decreases while equilibrium stays fixed because mass becomes smaller."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Neither changes due to gravity in the ideal horizontal model; T = 2π√(m/k) and horizontal equilibrium is set by the spring.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"shm-21","topic":"shm","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reviewed-shm","q":"A pendulum clock is moved to a location with weaker gravitational field strength. Explain how its timing changes.","answers":["Weaker g increases the small-angle period, so a clock calibrated at stronger g runs slow.","Weaker g decreases the small-angle period, so a clock calibrated at stronger g runs fast.","Weaker g reduces weight and inertia equally, so the clock's period remains unchanged."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Its period increases according to T = 2π√(l/g), so it completes fewer swings and runs slow.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
];

// ---------- AS91524 exam-calibrated core ----------
// These questions map directly to Explanatory Note 4 of the achievement
// standard. They are prioritised by the draw engine; the bank above remains
// available as extension/fallback material once a core pool is exhausted.
const NCEA_EXAM_QUESTIONS = [
  // Translational motion: centre of mass, 2-D momentum and impulse
  { id: 'ncea-trans-01', exam: true, strand: 'centre-of-mass-1d', topic: 'com', level: 1, q: 'Two carved stones lie on a light beam. A 2.0 kg stone is at x = 0 and a 6.0 kg stone is at x = 4.0 m. Where is their centre of mass measured from x = 0?', answers: ['3.0 m, from x_COM = Σmx/Σm = (2.0×0 + 6.0×4.0)/8.0.', '2.0 m, because the centre of mass is always the midpoint.', '1.0 m, because the heavier stone pulls the centre one quarter of the way from itself.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-trans-02', exam: true, strand: 'centre-of-mass-2d', topic: 'com', level: 2, q: 'Three supply packs are treated as point masses: 2.0 kg at (0,0), 3.0 kg at (4.0,0), and 5.0 kg at (0,6.0), with positions in metres. Which centre-of-mass position is correct?', answers: ['(1.20 m, 3.00 m), found by applying Σmx/Σm separately in x and y.', '(0.80 m, 2.00 m), found by dividing each coordinate sum by three packs.', '(2.00 m, 3.00 m), the geometric centre of their triangular arrangement.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-trans-03', exam: true, strand: 'momentum-2d', topic: 'momentum', level: 2, q: 'A 3.0 kg dart frog moves east at 4.0 m s⁻¹ while a 4.0 kg parrot moves north at 3.0 m s⁻¹. What is the total momentum of the two-animal system?', answers: ['17.0 kg m s⁻¹ at 45° north of east, because the perpendicular components are 12 and 12 kg m s⁻¹.', '24.0 kg m s⁻¹ north-east, because the two momentum magnitudes add directly.', '0 kg m s⁻¹, because equal perpendicular momenta cancel.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-trans-04', exam: true, strand: 'impulse-2d', topic: 'momentum', level: 2, q: 'Take east as positive. A 0.20 kg seed travels east at 8.0 m s⁻¹, rebounds west at 4.0 m s⁻¹, and is in contact with a wall for 0.030 s. Which impulse and average force act on the seed?', answers: ['J = −2.4 N s and F_avg = −80 N; the negative signs show both changes are westward.', 'J = −0.80 N s and F_avg = −26.7 N; only the final velocity contributes.', 'J = +2.4 N s and F_avg = +80 N; impulse uses speed and cannot be negative.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-trans-05', exam: true, strand: 'momentum-2d-collision', topic: 'momentum', level: 3, q: 'A 2.0 kg cart moving east at 6.0 m s⁻¹ sticks to a 3.0 kg cart moving north at 4.0 m s⁻¹. Which result correctly applies vector momentum conservation?', answers: ['The joined carts move at 3.39 m s⁻¹, 45° north of east: p = (12,12) kg m s⁻¹ and v = p/(5.0 kg).', 'They move at 2.40 m s⁻¹ north-east: each momentum component is divided by the original cart’s mass.', 'They stop because equal east and north momentum components cancel after an inelastic collision.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-trans-06', exam: true, strand: 'impulse-graph', topic: 'momentum', level: 2, q: 'A force–time graph for a vine catching an explorer is triangular, rising from zero to 800 N and returning to zero over 0.150 s. What momentum change does its area represent?', answers: ['60.0 kg m s⁻¹, because J = ½ × 0.150 × 800.', '120 kg m s⁻¹, because impulse is peak force multiplied by total time.', '5.33×10³ kg m s⁻¹, because impulse is force divided by collision time.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-trans-07', exam: true, strand: 'centre-of-mass-motion', topic: 'com', level: 3, q: 'A seed pod follows a projectile path and explodes into fragments. Air resistance is negligible. Which explanation fully describes the centre of mass after the explosion?', answers: ['It continues along the same projectile path because explosion forces are internal; the only external force is gravity, so a_COM remains g downward.', 'It follows the heaviest fragment because that fragment carries most of the mass and therefore defines the system motion.', 'It drops vertically from the explosion point because the explosion removes the pod’s original horizontal momentum.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-trans-08', exam: true, strand: 'momentum-energy', topic: 'momentum', level: 3, q: 'Two carts collide in an isolated system and lock together. Which comparison of momentum and kinetic energy is comprehensive?', answers: ['Vector momentum is conserved because external impulse is negligible; kinetic energy decreases because deformation, sound, and thermal energy receive part of the initial kinetic energy.', 'Both momentum and kinetic energy decrease by the same percentage because the carts share a lower final speed.', 'Kinetic energy is conserved because total energy is conserved, but momentum is lost when the carts deform.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-trans-09', exam: true, strand: 'impulse-components', topic: 'momentum', level: 3, q: 'A 0.50 kg fruit initially moves east at 10 m s⁻¹. A bat gives it an impulse of 6.0 N s north. What final velocity follows from the vector change in momentum?', answers: ['v = (10 east, 12 north) m s⁻¹, so its speed is 15.6 m s⁻¹ at 50.2° north of east.', 'v = 22 m s⁻¹ north-east, because 10 m s⁻¹ and 12 m s⁻¹ add as scalars.', 'v = 12 m s⁻¹ north, because the impulse replaces the original eastward momentum.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-trans-10', exam: true, strand: 'centre-of-mass-velocity', topic: 'com', level: 2, q: 'A 2.0 kg monkey runs east at 3.0 m s⁻¹ on a 6.0 kg raft drifting west at 1.0 m s⁻¹. What is the centre-of-mass velocity of the monkey–raft system?', answers: ['0 m s⁻¹, because total momentum is (2.0×3.0) + (6.0×−1.0) = 0.', '1.0 m s⁻¹ east, because their velocities average to +1.0 m s⁻¹.', '2.0 m s⁻¹ east, because the monkey moves 2.0 m s⁻¹ faster than the raft.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-trans-11', exam: true, strand: 'external-impulse', topic: 'momentum', level: 3, q: 'The monkey in the previous system turns around by pushing on the raft while horizontal external forces are negligible. Why does the system centre-of-mass velocity remain unchanged?', answers: ['The push forces form an internal Newton’s-third-law pair, so their impulses cancel for the system and total momentum remains constant.', 'The monkey and raft must keep their individual momenta unchanged because there is no external force.', 'The centre of mass stays fixed only because the initial total momentum happened to be zero; momentum conservation would otherwise fail.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-trans-12', exam: true, strand: 'force-time-comparison', topic: 'momentum', level: 3, q: 'Two safety vines stop identical falling explorers from the same speed. Vine A stops its explorer in 0.20 s; Vine B stops theirs in 0.50 s. Compare the impulses and average forces.', answers: ['The impulses have equal magnitude because Δp is the same; Vine B’s average force is 0.20/0.50 = 0.40 times Vine A’s.', 'Vine B supplies 2.5 times the impulse because it acts for 2.5 times as long.', 'The forces are equal because both explorers undergo the same change in momentum.'], correct: 0 },

  // Rotating systems: angular kinematics, torque, inertia, momentum and energy
  { id: 'ncea-rot-01', exam: true, strand: 'angular-kinematics', topic: 'rotational', level: 1, q: 'A temple wheel starts from rest and accelerates uniformly at 0.80 rad s⁻² for 5.0 s. What angular speed does it reach?', answers: ['4.0 rad s⁻¹, from ω_f = ω_i + αt.', '3.2 rad s⁻¹, from ω = αt².', '10 rad s⁻¹, from θ = ½αt².'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-rot-02', exam: true, strand: 'angular-kinematics-linked', topic: 'rotational', level: 2, q: 'The same wheel starts from rest with α = 0.80 rad s⁻² for 5.0 s. Through what angle does it rotate and how many revolutions is this?', answers: ['10.0 rad, or 1.59 revolutions, using θ = ½αt² and N = θ/(2π).', '20.0 rad, or 3.18 revolutions, using θ = ω_ft.', '2.0 rad, or 0.318 revolutions, using θ = αt/2.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-rot-03', exam: true, strand: 'torque-components', topic: 'rotational', level: 2, q: 'A 40 N force acts on a lever 0.60 m from its pivot at 30° to the lever. What torque magnitude does it produce?', answers: ['12 N m, because τ = rF sin30° and only the perpendicular force component turns the lever.', '24 N m, because τ = rF regardless of force direction.', '20.8 N m, because the parallel component F cos30° produces the rotation.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-rot-04', exam: true, strand: 'net-torque', topic: 'rotational', level: 2, q: 'A gate experiences 30 N m anticlockwise and 18 N m clockwise torques. Its rotational inertia is 4.0 kg m². Taking anticlockwise as positive, what is its angular acceleration?', answers: ['+3.0 rad s⁻², because τ_net = 12 N m and α = τ_net/I.', '+12 rad s⁻², because angular acceleration equals net torque.', '+7.5 rad s⁻², because only the larger torque is divided by inertia.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-rot-05', exam: true, strand: 'rotational-inertia', topic: 'rotational', level: 2, q: 'Two 3.0 kg carvings are attached to a light rod, each 0.80 m from the rotation axis. What is their total rotational inertia?', answers: ['3.84 kg m², from I = Σmr² = 2(3.0)(0.80²).', '4.80 kg m², from I = Σmr.', '1.92 kg m², because the two equal masses count as one rotating object.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-rot-06', exam: true, strand: 'torque-inertia-linked', topic: 'rotational', level: 3, q: 'The carvings are moved from 0.80 m to 0.40 m from the axis while the same net torque acts. How does their angular acceleration change?', answers: ['It becomes four times larger: I ∝ r² falls to one quarter, and α = τ/I.', 'It doubles because halving radius halves rotational inertia.', 'It becomes one quarter as large because objects closer to the axis rotate less easily.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-rot-07', exam: true, strand: 'angular-momentum', topic: 'rotational', level: 2, q: 'A 2.0 kg fruit moving tangentially at 5.0 m s⁻¹ lands on the rim of a stationary platform of radius 1.5 m and inertia 12 kg m². What shared angular speed results?', answers: ['1.11 rad s⁻¹, since L_i = mvr = 15 and I_f = 12 + mr² = 16.5 kg m².', '1.25 rad s⁻¹, because the fruit’s inertia is not part of the final rotating system.', '3.33 rad s⁻¹, because angular speed is simply v/r.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-rot-08', exam: true, strand: 'angular-momentum-energy', topic: 'rotational', level: 3, q: 'An explorer on a freely rotating platform pulls two masses inward. Which linked account is correct?', answers: ['External torque is negligible, so L = Iω is conserved; I decreases and ω increases, while rotational kinetic energy increases because the explorer does work pulling inward.', 'Angular momentum and kinetic energy are both conserved, so neither I nor ω can change.', 'The inward pull supplies an external torque, increasing angular momentum and leaving kinetic energy unchanged.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-rot-09', exam: true, strand: 'rotational-energy', topic: 'rotational', level: 2, q: 'A flywheel has I = 5.0 kg m² and ω = 8.0 rad s⁻¹. How much rotational kinetic energy does it store?', answers: ['160 J, from E_K(rot) = ½Iω².', '40 J, from E = Iω.', '320 J, because the factor ½ is used only for translational energy.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-rot-10', exam: true, strand: 'energy-angular-speed', topic: 'rotational', level: 3, q: 'A constant motor torque does 600 J of work on a wheel whose inertia is 3.0 kg m². The wheel starts from rest and losses are negligible. What angular speed results?', answers: ['20.0 rad s⁻¹, because W = ΔE_K = ½Iω².', '14.1 rad s⁻¹, because ω = √(W/I).', '200 rad s⁻¹, because angular speed equals work divided by inertia.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-rot-11', exam: true, strand: 'angular-braking', topic: 'rotational', level: 3, q: 'A wheel slows uniformly from 12 rad s⁻¹ to rest through 36 rad. Its inertia is 4.5 kg m². Which braking torque is correct?', answers: ['−9.0 N m: α = (0²−12²)/(2×36) = −2.0 rad s⁻², then τ = Iα.', '−18 N m: angular displacement must first be converted to revolutions.', '−54 N m: torque equals inertia multiplied by initial angular speed.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-rot-12', exam: true, strand: 'force-torque-work', topic: 'rotational', level: 3, q: 'A tangential force at a wheel’s rim produces a constant torque. Which chain correctly links the interaction to the wheel’s motion and energy?', answers: ['The force gives τ = rF; τ/I gives α; angular kinematics gives ω; and work τθ equals the increase in ½Iω² if losses are negligible.', 'The force gives α = rF directly; multiplying α by θ gives angular momentum; angular momentum then equals kinetic energy.', 'The torque fixes ω immediately, so neither time nor angular displacement is needed to determine the motion.'], correct: 0 },

  // Circular motion and gravity
  { id: 'ncea-circ-01', exam: true, strand: 'multi-force-circle', topic: 'circular', level: 2, q: 'A 600 kg cart crosses the top of a circular hill of radius 20 m at 10 m s⁻¹. What normal force acts on it? Use g = 9.81 m s⁻².', answers: ['2.89×10³ N upward, because mg − N = mv²/r.', '8.89×10³ N upward, because N + mg = mv²/r.', '3.00×10³ N downward, because the normal force supplies all centripetal force.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-circ-02', exam: true, strand: 'vertical-circle', topic: 'circular', level: 3, q: 'A mass on a vine moves in a vertical circle. Compare the vine tension at the bottom and top when the speed is the same at both positions.', answers: ['T_bottom − mg = mv²/r while T_top + mg = mv²/r, so T_bottom exceeds T_top by 2mg.', 'Tension is identical because mv²/r is identical at both positions.', 'T_top exceeds T_bottom by 2mg because gravity opposes centripetal force at the top.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-circ-03', exam: true, strand: 'banked-circle', topic: 'circular', level: 3, q: 'A frictionless banked track is designed for speed v and radius r. Which derivation gives its bank angle θ?', answers: ['N cosθ = mg and N sinθ = mv²/r, so tanθ = v²/(rg).', 'N sinθ = mg and N cosθ = mv²/r, so tanθ = rg/v².', 'N = mg and friction supplies mv²/r, so the bank angle is irrelevant.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-circ-04', exam: true, strand: 'conical-pendulum', topic: 'circular', level: 3, q: 'A conical pendulum has string angle θ from vertical. Which pair of equations correctly separates the forces?', answers: ['T cosθ = mg vertically and T sinθ = mv²/r horizontally.', 'T sinθ = mg vertically and T cosθ = mv²/r horizontally.', 'T − mg = mv²/r because all forces act along the string.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-grav-01', exam: true, strand: 'gravitation', topic: 'orbital', level: 2, q: 'At distance r from a planet’s centre, gravitational force is F. What is the force at distance 3r, with masses unchanged?', answers: ['F/9, because F_g = GMm/r².', 'F/3, because gravitational force is inversely proportional to distance.', '9F, because the larger orbit requires a larger centripetal force.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-grav-02', exam: true, strand: 'satellite-speed', topic: 'orbital', level: 3, q: 'For a satellite of mass m in a circular orbit of radius r about mass M, which derivation gives orbital speed?', answers: ['Set GMm/r² = mv²/r; m cancels and v = √(GM/r).', 'Set GMm/r² = mg; M cancels and v = √(gr).', 'Set GMm/r² = ½mv²; r cancels and v = √(2GM).'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-grav-03', exam: true, strand: 'satellite-period', topic: 'orbital', level: 3, q: 'Two satellites orbit the same planet at radii r and 4r. Compare their periods.', answers: ['T_4r = 8T_r, because T² = 4π²r³/(GM), so T ∝ r³ᐟ².', 'T_4r = 4T_r, because the outer path is four times larger.', 'T_4r = 2T_r, because orbital speed is halved at 4r.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-grav-04', exam: true, strand: 'apparent-weight', topic: 'orbital', level: 3, q: 'An astronaut feels weightless in a low circular orbit where g is still about 8.7 m s⁻². Which explanation is complete?', answers: ['Gravity provides the centripetal force and accelerates craft and astronaut together; with no supporting normal force, apparent weight is zero even though gravitational weight is not.', 'Gravity is balanced by an equal outward centrifugal force, so both net force and acceleration are zero.', 'The astronaut is beyond Earth’s gravitational field, so no force acts on them.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-grav-05', exam: true, strand: 'orbit-scaling', topic: 'orbital', level: 2, q: 'A satellite moves from circular radius r to circular radius 9r around the same planet. How do its orbital speed and period compare?', answers: ['Its speed becomes v/3 and its period becomes 27T.', 'Its speed becomes v/9 and its period becomes 9T.', 'Its speed becomes 3v and its period becomes 27T.'], correct: 0 },

  // Oscillating systems: SHM equations, phasors, energy, damping and resonance
  { id: 'ncea-shm-01', exam: true, strand: 'shm-condition', topic: 'shm', level: 2, q: 'Which condition establishes that a moving object is in simple harmonic motion?', answers: ['Its acceleration is proportional to displacement from equilibrium and oppositely directed: a = −ω²y.', 'Its speed is proportional to displacement and always directed toward equilibrium.', 'Its restoring force is constant throughout the motion.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-shm-02', exam: true, strand: 'spring-period', topic: 'shm', level: 2, q: 'A 0.50 kg mass oscillates on a spring with k = 200 N m⁻¹. What is its period?', answers: ['0.314 s, from T = 2π√(m/k).', '2.51 s, from T = 2π√(k/m).', '0.050 s, from T = m/k.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-shm-03', exam: true, strand: 'shm-equations', topic: 'shm', level: 2, q: 'An oscillator follows y = 0.080 cos(4.0t), with SI units. What are its maximum speed and maximum acceleration?', answers: ['v_max = Aω = 0.320 m s⁻¹ and a_max = Aω² = 1.28 m s⁻².', 'v_max = 0.020 m s⁻¹ and a_max = 0.0050 m s⁻².', 'v_max = 0.320 m s⁻¹ and a_max = 0.320 m s⁻² because both depend on Aω.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-shm-04', exam: true, strand: 'shm-state', topic: 'shm', level: 3, q: 'For y = A cosωt, what are velocity and acceleration when y = +A/2 and the oscillator is moving toward equilibrium?', answers: ['v = −(√3/2)Aω and a = −(1/2)Aω²; both signs follow from the chosen positive direction.', 'v = +(1/2)Aω and a = +(√3/2)Aω² because y is positive.', 'v = 0 and a = −Aω² because any positive displacement is a turning point.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-shm-05', exam: true, strand: 'phasor-time', topic: 'shm', level: 3, q: 'An oscillator starts at +A and first reaches y = −A/2. What fraction of a period has elapsed?', answers: ['One third of a period, because cos(ωt) = −1/2 first at ωt = 2π/3.', 'One sixth of a period, because the displacement has changed by half an amplitude.', 'One half of a period, because all negative displacements occur after T/2.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-shm-06', exam: true, strand: 'shm-energy', topic: 'shm', level: 3, q: 'At displacement y = A/2 in ideal spring SHM, what fractions of the total energy are elastic potential and kinetic?', answers: ['E_P/E_total = y²/A² = 1/4, so E_K/E_total = 3/4.', 'Each is 1/2 because the displacement is half the amplitude.', 'E_P is 3/4 and E_K is 1/4 because the oscillator is closer to a turning point.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-shm-07', exam: true, strand: 'pendulum-geometry', topic: 'shm', level: 2, q: 'A 1.50 m pendulum is released from 6.0° to the vertical. Using the small-angle model, what is its displacement amplitude along the horizontal direction?', answers: ['0.157 m, from A = l sin6.0°.', '0.00914 m, from A = l(6.0π/180)².', '9.00 m, from A = lθ with θ left in degrees.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-shm-08', exam: true, strand: 'damping', topic: 'shm', level: 3, q: 'How do light damping and critical damping differ after an oscillator is displaced and released?', answers: ['Light damping produces oscillations of decreasing amplitude; critical damping returns to equilibrium without oscillating in the shortest possible time.', 'Both oscillate with constant amplitude, but critical damping has a longer period.', 'Light damping prevents any oscillation, while critical damping produces slowly decreasing oscillations.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-shm-09', exam: true, strand: 'driven-resonance', topic: 'shm', level: 3, q: 'Why does a lightly damped oscillator build a large amplitude when driven near its natural frequency?', answers: ['The driver transfers energy efficiently with the correct phase each cycle; amplitude grows until energy input per cycle balances damping losses.', 'The driving force cancels the restoring force, so the object moves with no acceleration.', 'The natural frequency increases to match every driving frequency, conserving amplitude.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-shm-10', exam: true, strand: 'frequency-change', topic: 'shm', level: 3, q: 'A spring oscillator’s mass is quadrupled while k and amplitude remain unchanged. Compare its period, maximum speed, and total energy.', answers: ['Period doubles, maximum speed halves because ω halves, and total energy ½kA² is unchanged.', 'Period halves, maximum speed doubles, and total energy quadruples with mass.', 'Period doubles, while maximum speed and total energy both remain unchanged.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-shm-11', exam: true, strand: 'shm-graph', topic: 'shm', level: 3, q: 'A displacement–time graph passes downward through y = 0. What must be true at that instant for ideal SHM?', answers: ['Velocity has its maximum negative value, acceleration is zero, and all mechanical energy is kinetic.', 'Velocity is zero, acceleration is maximum negative, and all energy is elastic potential.', 'Both velocity and acceleration are zero because the oscillator is at equilibrium.'], correct: 0 },
  { id: 'ncea-shm-12', exam: true, strand: 'resonance-damping-curve', topic: 'shm', level: 3, q: 'Compared with light damping, how does greater damping change an amplitude–driving-frequency resonance curve?', answers: ['It lowers and broadens the peak, so the response is less selective and the maximum amplitude is smaller.', 'It raises and narrows the peak because more force is required to maintain resonance.', 'It shifts every point to zero amplitude except at the unchanged natural frequency.'], correct: 0 }
];

// Written-response theory core. These deliberately avoid numerical substitution:
// the player must identify the complete explanation, comparison, or derivation
// that would earn credit in an AS91524 written response.
const NCEA_THEORY_QUESTIONS = [
  // Translational motion
  {"id":"ncea-theory-trans-01","topic":"com","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"centre-of-mass-1d","q":"A light beam carries unequal masses at its ends. Explain, rather than calculate, why its centre of mass is closer to the heavier mass.","answers":["Mass-weighted position gives the heavier mass the shorter balancing distance.","Mass-weighted position gives the heavier mass the longer balancing distance.","Mass-weighted position is halfway along any beam whose own mass is negligible."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Centre of mass is a mass-weighted position. For the moments about the centre of mass to balance, the heavier mass must have the shorter distance.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-trans-02","topic":"com","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"centre-of-mass-2d","q":"Describe how the centre of mass of several objects in a plane can be justified from their positions without treating the arrangement as one-dimensional.","answers":["Find mass-weighted x and y coordinates separately, then combine them into one point.","Find the mean distance from the origin and assign it the direction of the largest mass.","Find unweighted x and y coordinates separately, since averaging already accounts for mass."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Resolve every position into x and y components, form a mass-weighted mean in each direction, then combine those independent coordinates into one point.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-trans-03","topic":"momentum","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"momentum-2d","q":"Why must momenta at right angles be combined vectorially rather than by adding their magnitudes?","answers":["Perpendicular components add independently; direction cannot be discarded when combining them.","Perpendicular components cancel each other because neither lies along the other's direction.","Perpendicular components add as magnitudes because momentum becomes scalar during impact."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Momentum is a vector, so perpendicular components describe independent directions; a vector diagram or component addition is required to obtain both resultant magnitude and direction.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-trans-04","topic":"momentum","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"impulse-vector","q":"A ball strikes a wall and rebounds. Explain why using only the initial and final speeds gives an incomplete impulse argument.","answers":["Momentum change is a vector difference, so initial and final directions both matter.","Momentum change is the difference of speeds, so direction does not affect the impulse.","Momentum change is the final momentum alone, since the wall removes all initial momentum."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Impulse equals the vector change p_final − p_initial. Reversal changes the sign of velocity, so the momentum change depends on both directions, not merely the difference between speed magnitudes.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-trans-05","topic":"momentum","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"impulse-graph","q":"Justify why the area under a force–time graph gives change in momentum, even when force varies during the collision.","answers":["Summing small FΔt contributions gives impulse and therefore the vector momentum change.","Summing small FΔt contributions gives work and therefore the change in kinetic energy.","Summing small FΔt contributions gives acceleration and therefore the final velocity."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Each narrow strip FΔt is a small impulse; summing the strips gives total impulse, which equals Δp.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-trans-06","topic":"momentum","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"collision-conservation","q":"Explain why momentum can be conserved in an inelastic collision even though kinetic energy is not.","answers":["Internal force pairs preserve total momentum while kinetic energy changes into other forms.","Internal force pairs preserve kinetic energy while some total momentum is lost as heat.","Internal force pairs preserve each object's momentum while total kinetic energy is conserved."],"correct":0,"explanation":"With negligible external impulse, internal collision forces cancel in equal-and-opposite pairs, preserving total momentum; kinetic energy may transform into deformation, heat, and sound while total energy remains conserved.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-trans-07","topic":"com","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"centre-of-mass-motion","q":"An airborne object splits into fragments while external horizontal impulse is negligible. What constrains the fragments' horizontal momenta?","answers":["With zero external horizontal impulse, the fragments' horizontal momenta sum to the original.","With zero external horizontal impulse, every fragment retains the original horizontal velocity.","With zero external horizontal impulse, the largest fragment retains all the original momentum."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Horizontal momentum of the complete system is conserved because external horizontal impulse is negligible. Individual fragment velocities can change, and unequal masses mean their velocities need not match.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-trans-08","topic":"momentum","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"system-boundary","q":"Why must a momentum-conservation explanation clearly define the system boundary?","answers":["Only the chosen complete system can be tested for negligible net external impulse.","Only objects initially moving belong inside a system used for momentum conservation.","Only objects remaining in contact belong inside a system used for momentum conservation."],"correct":0,"explanation":"A force is internal or external only relative to the chosen system; conservation requires negligible net external impulse on that complete system.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-trans-09","topic":"momentum","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"collision-force-time","q":"Two safety materials stop the same moving object, but one takes longer. Give the complete impulse explanation for the different average forces.","answers":["Equal initial and final momenta mean equal impulse; longer time lowers average force.","Equal initial and final momenta mean less impulse when the stopping time increases.","Equal initial and final momenta mean equal average force regardless of stopping time."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The initial and final momenta are the same in both stops, so the impulses are equal; spreading that fixed Δp over a longer Δt reduces the average force through F_avg = Δp/Δt.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-trans-10","topic":"com","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"centre-of-mass-internal-forces","q":"People rearrange themselves on a raft already drifting uniformly, with negligible horizontal external force. What happens to the combined centre-of-mass velocity?","answers":["A drifting system centre retains its initial velocity despite internal rearrangements.","A drifting system centre comes to rest whenever the people stop moving relative to the raft.","A drifting system centre accelerates toward whichever person has the greatest walking speed."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Their pushes are internal force pairs, so the raft and people exchange momentum while total momentum stays constant; only an external resultant force could accelerate the centre of mass.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},

  // Rotating systems
  {"id":"ncea-theory-rot-01","topic":"rotational","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"angular-kinematics","q":"An angular-velocity–time graph passes from positive to negative values. How can its net angular displacement be found?","answers":["The area under an angular-velocity–time graph gives signed angular displacement.","The gradient of an angular-velocity–time graph gives signed angular displacement.","The height of an angular-velocity–time graph gives accumulated angular displacement."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Signed area under ω versus time gives angular displacement. Areas below the time axis subtract from those above; adding their magnitudes instead gives total angular distance.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-rot-02","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"angular-linear-link","q":"Prove why points farther from the axis have greater linear speed on the same rigid rotating object.","answers":["Equal angular motion gives different arc distances, so linear speed increases with radius.","Equal angular motion gives equal arc distances, so linear speed is independent of radius.","Equal angular motion gives smaller arc distances outward, so speed decreases with radius."],"correct":0,"explanation":"All points sweep the same angular displacement in the same time, but arc length is d = rθ; therefore v = d/t = rω and greater r gives greater v.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-rot-03","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"torque","q":"Explain why only the component of a force perpendicular to the lever arm produces torque.","answers":["The radial component has no moment arm; the perpendicular component gives rF sinθ.","The perpendicular component has no moment arm; the radial component gives rF cosθ.","Both components have the same moment arm, so their magnitudes add to give rF."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The parallel component acts through the axis and has no turning moment; resolving the force perpendicular gives τ = rF⊥ = rF sinθ.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-rot-04","topic":"rotational","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"net-torque","q":"A wheel has clockwise and anticlockwise torques acting simultaneously. What must a complete prediction of its angular acceleration include?","answers":["Add torques with signs, then divide the resultant by I to obtain angular acceleration.","Add torque magnitudes, then divide the total by I regardless of their turning directions.","Add torque magnitudes, then multiply by I because greater inertia gives greater acceleration."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Choose a sign convention, add torques with direction to find τ_net, then use α = τ_net/I; the direction of α follows the sign of the resultant torque.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-rot-05","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"rotational-inertia","q":"Two objects have equal mass and radius, but one has more mass near its rim. Explain their different responses to the same torque.","answers":["Rim-weighting raises I; the same net torque then produces a smaller angular acceleration.","Rim-weighting lowers I; the same net torque then produces a larger angular acceleration.","Rim-weighting leaves I fixed; the same mass always gives the same angular acceleration."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The rim-weighted object has larger I because mass contributions scale with r²; from α = τ/I it has the smaller angular acceleration.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-rot-06","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"angular-momentum","q":"A rotating person pulls masses toward the axis. Link every step needed to explain the increase in angular speed.","answers":["Moving mass inward lowers I, so conserving Iω requires angular speed to rise.","Moving mass inward raises I, so conserving Iω requires angular speed to rise.","Moving mass inward fixes ω, so conserving Iω requires angular momentum to fall."],"correct":0,"explanation":"No external torque means angular momentum is conserved; moving mass inward reduces I; since L = Iω stays constant, ω must increase.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-rot-07","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"angular-momentum-energy","q":"A spinning person pulls held masses inward with negligible external torque. Why can rotational kinetic energy increase?","answers":["Internal work transfers chemical energy to rotation while angular momentum remains fixed.","Internal work supplies new angular momentum while total rotational kinetic energy stays fixed.","Zero external torque guarantees zero work, so rotational kinetic energy cannot increase."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Internal work during the pull transfers chemical energy into rotational kinetic energy. Angular momentum remains fixed, but rotational kinetic energy alone is not conserved; at fixed L, K = L²/(2I) rises as I falls.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-rot-08","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"capture-angular-momentum","q":"A small object lands tangentially at radius r on a stationary turntable and sticks. Which angular-momentum equation applies about its fixed axis?","answers":["Initial mvr equals final (I_table + mr²)ω if external angular impulse is negligible.","Initial mv equals final I_tableω because the captured object's inertia no longer matters.","Initial kinetic energy equals final rotational energy because sticking conserves both."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Its initial angular momentum mvr about the axis equals the final (I_turntable + mr²)ω when external torque impulse is negligible.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-rot-09","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"rotational-work-energy","q":"For fixed I and constant net torque, how can rotational work be connected symbolically to the change in kinetic energy?","answers":["Combine τθ with τ = Iα and ω_f² − ω_i² = 2αθ to obtain the energy change.","Combine τt with τ = Iα and ω_f − ω_i = αt to obtain the kinetic energy change.","Combine τ/θ with τ = Iα and ω_f² − ω_i² = 2αθ to obtain the energy change."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Rotational work is W = τθ; using τ = Iα and the angular kinematic relation links this work to Δ(½Iω²).","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-rot-10","topic":"rotational","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"angular-momentum-direction","q":"Why must angular momentum be treated with direction when two rotating parts join?","answers":["Opposite rotations have opposite signed angular momenta about the common axis.","Opposite rotations have positive angular momenta because inertia has no direction.","Opposite rotations always cancel angular momentum regardless of speed or inertia."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Angular momentum is a vector along the rotation axis, so opposite senses of rotation have opposite signs and must be combined before applying L_i = L_f.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-rot-11","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"constant-angular-acceleration","q":"A wheel's angular-velocity–time graph is a straight sloping line. Which interpretation distinguishes acceleration from displacement?","answers":["A straight sloping ω–t line indicates constant α, while its area measures displacement.","A straight sloping ω–t line indicates constant ω, while its gradient measures displacement.","A straight sloping ω–t line indicates changing α, while its height measures displacement."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The gradient dω/dt is constant angular acceleration. The signed area under ω(t) is angular displacement. A sloping straight line does not indicate constant angular velocity.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-rot-12","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"torque-components","q":"A force of unchanged magnitude is redirected so its line of action passes nearer the rotation axis. What happens to torque magnitude?","answers":["Moving the force line nearer the axis reduces torque even if the force magnitude stays fixed.","Moving the force line nearer the axis raises torque even if the force magnitude stays fixed.","Moving the force line nearer the axis leaves torque fixed if the application point is unchanged."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Torque magnitude is force times perpendicular distance from the axis to its line of action. Changing direction can reduce that distance without changing the point where the force is applied.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-rot-13","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"rotational-inertia-distribution","q":"Use the structure I = Σmr² to explain why rotational inertia depends on the chosen axis as well as the object.","answers":["Different axes change the distances in Σmr², so the same object can have different I.","Different axes change total mass, so the same object has different inertia only if mass changes.","Different axes leave every distance unchanged, so inertia is fixed for the same rigid object."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Changing the axis changes each mass element’s perpendicular distance r; because every contribution is weighted by r², the same object can strongly resist rotation about one axis and weakly resist it about another.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-rot-14","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"angular-impulse","q":"Develop the rotational analogue of the impulse–momentum relationship.","answers":["Signed area under torque versus time gives angular impulse and therefore ΔL.","Signed area under torque versus angle gives angular impulse and therefore ΔL.","Signed gradient of torque versus time gives angular impulse and therefore ΔL."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Since net torque is the rate of change of angular momentum, a torque acting over time gives angular impulse ∫τ dt = ΔL; the area under a torque–time graph therefore gives the change in angular momentum.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-rot-15","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"inelastic-rotational-collision","q":"Two coaxial discs rotating at different angular velocities are pressed together and settle at one shared angular velocity. Explain what is and is not conserved.","answers":["Total angular momentum is conserved, but internal friction converts kinetic energy to heat.","Total kinetic energy is conserved, but internal friction removes angular momentum as heat.","Both totals are conserved separately, because internal friction cannot change either quantity."],"correct":0,"explanation":"With negligible external torque, total angular momentum is conserved; friction between the discs is internal, but it transfers some rotational kinetic energy into thermal energy, so rotational kinetic energy is not conserved.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-rot-16","topic":"rotational","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"rotational-power-energy","q":"Connect torque, angular displacement, and rotational kinetic energy in an explanation of how a motor speeds up a flywheel.","answers":["Motor work raises rotational energy; net torque determines angular acceleration.","Motor torque raises rotational energy without work, since angular displacement is unnecessary.","Motor work raises angular momentum without torque, since inertia alone sets acceleration."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The motor’s torque does work W = ∫τ dθ; that transferred energy increases ½Iω², while the net torque also produces angular acceleration through τ_net = Iα.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},

  // Circular motion and gravity
  {"id":"ncea-theory-circ-01","topic":"circular","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"resultant-centripetal-force","q":"Why is “centripetal force” not an extra force to add to a free-body diagram?","answers":["It names the inward resultant of real forces, not an additional interaction.","It names a separate inward interaction that must be added to tension and weight.","It names an outward reaction that cancels the inward forces during circular motion."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Centripetal force names the inward resultant required for circular acceleration; real forces such as weight, tension, friction, or normal force combine to provide that resultant.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-circ-02","topic":"circular","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"vertical-circle-forces","q":"A stone on a taut string moves in a vertical circle under gravity. Taking inward as positive, compare its top and bottom radial force equations.","answers":["For an inward-tension string: top T + mg = mv_top²/r; bottom T − mg = mv_bottom²/r.","For an inward-tension string: top T − mg = mv_top²/r; bottom T + mg = mv_bottom²/r.","For an inward-tension string: top T = mg; bottom T = mg, because the radius stays fixed."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Take inward as positive at each position: at the top inward forces can add, while at the bottom an upward support/tension must exceed weight to leave the required inward resultant.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-circ-03","topic":"circular","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"apparent-weight-circle","q":"Explain why a rider can feel lighter at the top of a curved hill while gravitational field strength is essentially unchanged.","answers":["The downward resultant is mg − N, so the required radial acceleration makes N smaller.","The downward resultant is N − mg, so the required radial acceleration makes N larger.","The downward resultant is zero, so apparent weight falls only because gravity becomes weaker."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Apparent weight is the normal force. At the crest, the downward resultant supplies centripetal acceleration, so mg − N = mv²/r and N is less than mg.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-circ-04","topic":"circular","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"banked-circle","q":"Derive symbolically why a frictionless banked turn has tanθ = v²/(rg).","answers":["Divide N sinθ = mv²/r by N cosθ = mg to eliminate N and m.","Divide N cosθ = mv²/r by N sinθ = mg to eliminate N and m.","Divide N = mv²/r by N = mg because the force directions are identical."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Resolve N vertically and horizontally: N cosθ balances mg and N sinθ supplies mv²/r; dividing eliminates N and m, giving tanθ = v²/(rg).","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-circ-05","topic":"circular","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"conical-pendulum","q":"For a steady conical pendulum of fixed length l and angle θ from vertical, derive the angular-speed relationship.","answers":["Use T cosθ = mg and T sinθ = mω²l sinθ, giving cosθ = g/(ω²l).","Use T cosθ = mg and T sinθ = mω²l, giving cosθ independent of ω.","Use T = mg and T sinθ = mω²l sinθ, giving the same tension at every ω."],"correct":0,"explanation":"With radius l sinθ, the horizontal equation is T sinθ = mω²l sinθ. For a nonzero angle this gives T = mω²l. The vertical equation then gives cosθ = g/(ω²l).","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-grav-01","topic":"orbital","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"inverse-square-gravity","q":"For spherical masses outside each other's surfaces, what does the inverse-square dependence in Newton's gravitation law describe?","answers":["For fixed masses, gravitational force falls as the square of centre-to-centre distance grows.","For fixed masses, gravitational force falls as centre-to-surface distance grows linearly.","For fixed masses, gravitational force stays constant because both masses retain their weight."],"correct":0,"explanation":"For fixed masses, F = GMm/r² depends on centre-to-centre separation r. Increasing r reduces force by the square of the separation factor, not by the gap between surfaces.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-grav-02","topic":"orbital","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"satellite-speed-derivation","q":"Derive the dependence of circular-orbit speed on orbital radius without substituting numbers.","answers":["Equating GMm/r² with mv²/r cancels satellite mass and gives v² = GM/r.","Equating GMm/r² with mv cancels satellite mass and gives v = GM/r².","Equating GMm/r² with mv² cancels satellite mass and gives v² = GM/r²."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Set gravity equal to the required centripetal resultant: GMm/r² = mv²/r. Cancel m and one r to obtain v = √(GM/r), so larger circular orbits are slower.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-grav-03","topic":"orbital","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"satellite-period-derivation","q":"Show how circular-orbit dynamics leads to Kepler’s relationship T² ∝ r³ for satellites around the same central mass.","answers":["Substitute v = 2πr/T into the radial force equation to obtain T² proportional to r³.","Substitute v = r/T into an energy equation to obtain T proportional to r³.","Substitute constant v into the circumference relation to obtain T² proportional to r²."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Combine v = 2πr/T with GMm/r² = mv²/r; rearrangement gives T² = 4π²r³/(GM), so the proportionality follows when M is fixed.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-grav-04","topic":"orbital","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"orbital-weightlessness","q":"Distinguish true gravitational weight from apparent weight for an astronaut in orbit.","answers":["Gravity provides orbital acceleration while the absence of support gives zero apparent weight.","Gravity is absent in orbit while the spacecraft floor provides the required radial acceleration.","Gravity balances an outward contact force while the astronaut has zero resultant acceleration."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Gravity still acts and provides centripetal acceleration, but astronaut and spacecraft free-fall together; without a supporting normal force, apparent weight is zero.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-grav-05","topic":"orbital","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"satellite-mass","q":"Why does the mass of a satellite not affect its circular-orbit speed at a given radius?","answers":["Both gravitational force and required centripetal force scale with satellite mass.","Only gravitational force scales with satellite mass; the required centripetal force is fixed.","Only required centripetal force scales with satellite mass; gravitational force is fixed."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Satellite mass appears in both gravitational force GMm/r² and required centripetal force mv²/r, so it cancels when the forces are equated.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},

  // Oscillating systems
  {"id":"ncea-theory-shm-01","topic":"shm","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"shm-condition","q":"Why does a = −ω²y establish SHM when ω is a positive constant?","answers":["Constant positive ω² makes acceleration proportional to displacement and directed toward equilibrium.","Constant positive ω² makes acceleration proportional to velocity and directed against the motion.","Constant positive ω² makes acceleration constant throughout the path and directed toward equilibrium."],"correct":0,"explanation":"It shows acceleration is proportional to displacement and always directed oppositely toward equilibrium—the defining restoring condition for SHM.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-shm-02","topic":"shm","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"shm-phase","q":"Compare the phases of displacement, velocity, and acceleration in ideal SHM.","answers":["Acceleration opposes displacement; velocity is a quarter-cycle out of phase with displacement.","Acceleration matches displacement; velocity is a half-cycle out of phase with displacement.","Acceleration opposes velocity; displacement is a quarter-cycle out of phase with acceleration."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Acceleration is exactly opposite displacement, while velocity is a quarter-cycle out of phase with displacement and reaches extrema when displacement is zero.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-shm-03","topic":"shm","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"reference-circle","q":"Explain how a reference circle represents the displacement and velocity of an oscillator.","answers":["Project uniform circular motion onto a diameter to obtain the oscillator's motion.","Project accelerated circular motion onto a diameter to obtain constant-speed oscillation.","Follow the whole circular path because the oscillator physically travels around the reference circle."],"correct":0,"explanation":"SHM is the projection of uniform circular motion: the radius projection gives displacement, while the projected tangential velocity gives oscillator velocity and its phase relationship.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-shm-04","topic":"shm","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"shm-equations","q":"Starting from y = A cosωt, justify the signs in the corresponding velocity and acceleration equations.","answers":["Differentiating gives v = −Aω sinωt and a = −Aω² cosωt, toward equilibrium.","Differentiating gives v = Aω sinωt and a = Aω² cosωt, toward equilibrium.","Differentiating gives v = −A sinωt and a = −A cosωt, independent of frequency."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Differentiation gives v = −Aω sinωt and a = −Aω² cosωt = −ω²y; the negative acceleration sign expresses restoration toward equilibrium.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-shm-05","topic":"shm","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"shm-energy","q":"Describe energy changes during an ideal horizontal mass–spring cycle.","answers":["At the ends energy is elastic; at equilibrium it is kinetic; their sum stays constant.","At the ends energy is kinetic; at equilibrium it is elastic; their sum stays constant.","At the ends both energies peak; at equilibrium both vanish; their sum changes periodically."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Elastic potential energy is greatest at ±A and kinetic energy is zero; at equilibrium kinetic energy is greatest and elastic energy least; their sum remains constant without damping.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-shm-06","topic":"shm","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"amplitude-energy","q":"Prove how changing amplitude affects the total energy but not the period of an ideal horizontal mass–spring oscillator.","answers":["E = ½kA² changes with amplitude, while T = 2π√(m/k) does not.","E = ½kA² stays fixed with amplitude, while T = 2π√(m/k) increases.","E = ½kA² increases with amplitude, while T increases because the travel distance is greater."],"correct":0,"explanation":"E = ½kA², so energy depends on amplitude squared; T = 2π√(m/k) contains no A, so the period is unchanged within the ideal SHM model.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-shm-07","topic":"shm","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"damping-energy","q":"What do decreasing successive turning-point amplitudes indicate for a damped spring oscillator?","answers":["Successive turning-point amplitudes decrease as resistive work removes mechanical energy.","Successive turning-point amplitudes decrease because energy stays entirely kinetic after each cycle.","Successive turning-point amplitudes decrease while total mechanical energy remains constant."],"correct":0,"explanation":"At each turning point speed is zero, so the mechanical energy is ½kA_turning². Smaller successive turning amplitudes indicate mechanical energy loss through resistive work.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-shm-08","topic":"shm","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"driven-resonance","q":"Give an energy-based explanation for the large steady response near resonance in a lightly damped driven oscillator.","answers":["Near resonance the driver transfers energy efficiently; steady input balances damping loss.","Near resonance the restoring force disappears; amplitude grows without ongoing energy input.","Near resonance damping creates energy; the driver only determines the direction of motion."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Near resonance the driver adds energy efficiently through the force–velocity timing. Amplitude settles when average input balances damping loss; for a damped oscillator the displacement peak can lie slightly below the undamped natural frequency.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-shm-09","topic":"shm","level":3,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"resonance-curve","q":"How does increasing damping alter an amplitude-versus-driving-frequency resonance curve, and why?","answers":["Greater damping lowers and broadens the response peak because dissipation is stronger.","Greater damping raises and narrows the response peak because energy is more concentrated.","Greater damping leaves the peak unchanged because the natural frequency alone fixes amplitude."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The peak becomes lower and broader because more energy is dissipated each cycle, limiting maximum amplitude and reducing frequency selectivity.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
  {"id":"ncea-theory-shm-10","topic":"shm","level":2,"theory":true,"standard":"91524","journey":"mechanics","strand":"critical-damping","q":"A system is displaced and released from rest. Why is critical damping useful when oscillation is undesirable?","answers":["It returns from rest without oscillation as quickly as possible among non-oscillatory damping cases.","It returns from rest with many small oscillations as quickly as possible among all damping cases.","It returns from rest without any restoring force as quickly as possible by removing all inertia."],"correct":0,"explanation":"It returns the system to equilibrium without oscillation in the shortest possible time, avoiding repeated overshoot.","sourceIds":["as91524","schedule2025"]},
];

